1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,475
Chapter 15: Normal and Abnormal Fetal Anatomy.

3
00:00:06,475 --> 00:00:23,387
Biometry Brain And Spine Head, Face, And Neck Thorax Heart Gastrointestinal Obstruction Ventral Wall Defects Kidneys And Genitourinary Tract Skeleton References The ability to detect and characterize abnormalities before birth is one of the marvels of modern obstetrics.

4
00:00:23,387 --> 00:00:27,487
Sonography can image the fetus with remarkable precision.

5
00:00:27,487 --> 00:00:32,975
Components of the standard anatomic survey are listed in Table 15-1.

6
00:00:32,975 --> 00:00:45,737
These have been termed essential elements by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020) and minimal elements by the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (2018).

7
00:00:45,737 --> 00:00:54,300
Ideally, residency training in Obstetrics and Gynecology includes education in acquisition and interpretation of these views.

8
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:59,050
Resources provided by national societies assist with this endeavor .

9
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:01,362
Table 15-1.

10
00:01:01,362 --> 00:01:14,300
Components of Standard and Detailed Anatomic Surveys A detailed fetal anatomic survey is a specialized examination that may include more than 60 components, each determined on a case-by-case basis.

11
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:24,250
It is the primary method of evaluating at-risk pregnancies and characterizing fetal abnormalities, thereby aiding consultation and delivery planning.

12
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:31,887
Indications are shown in Table 14-6 and discussed further in Chapter 14 (p. 251).

13
00:01:31,887 --> 00:01:44,262
At Parkland hospital, the detailed survey includes, at minimum, an attempt to visualize all components required by the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine for normal detailed case submission .

14
00:01:44,262 --> 00:01:53,775
This chapter presents the standard and detailed anatomic surveys and some of the many fetal abnormalities that may be detected when visualization is optimal.

15
00:01:53,775 --> 00:02:03,250
When imaging or measuring any fetal structure, care should be taken to place the focal zone at the appropriate level and to magnify the image appropriately .

16
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:09,775
Whenever a fetal abnormality is identified, a detailed anatomic survey is recommended.

17
00:02:09,775 --> 00:02:16,175
With rare exception, amniocentesis with chromosomal microarray analysis also is offered.

18
00:02:16,175 --> 00:02:22,250
If a cardiac abnormality is identified, fetal echocardiography is indicated.

19
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:31,250
Additional indications for these specialized examinations and for fetal magnetic resonance (MR) imaging are reviewed in Chapter 14.

20
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:32,975
Biometry.

21
00:02:32,975 --> 00:02:43,200
In the first trimester, crown-rump length (CRL) measurement is used to establish or confirm gestational age (Appendix, p. 1234).

22
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:48,287
The earlier that sonography is performed, the more accurate this estimation.

23
00:02:48,287 --> 00:02:53,987
Gestational age assessment is reviewed in Chapter 14 (p. 248).

24
00:02:53,987 --> 00:03:01,850
The fetus is imaged in the midsagittal plane in a neutral, nonflexed position so that its length can be measured in a straight line .

25
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:04,950
The average of three measurements is used.

26
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:11,887
First-trimester nuchal translucency measurement is reviewed in Chapter 14 (p. 249).

27
00:03:11,887 --> 00:03:14,262
Figure 15-1.

28
00:03:14,262 --> 00:03:20,000
The crown-rump length measures 61 mm in this 12-week, 4-day fetus.

29
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:34,875
In the second and third trimesters, the biparietal diameter, head circumference, abdominal circumference, and femur length are measured to confirm gestational age, if not already established in the first trimester, and to estimate fetal weight.

30
00:03:34,875 --> 00:03:41,300
Ultrasound equipment and report packages calculate these estimates using standardized nomograms .

31
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:50,225
If an abnormality involving one of the parameters is suspected, consideration is given to excluding it from the gestational age calculation.

32
00:03:50,225 --> 00:04:00,075
Fetal weight nomograms are available that do not include the head measurements or the femur measurement (Hadlock, 1984; Shepard, 1982).

33
00:04:00,075 --> 00:04:05,612
The biparietal diameter and head circumference are measured in the transthalamic view.

34
00:04:05,612 --> 00:04:15,762
This is a transverse image that includes the midline falx cerebri, cavum septum pellucidum, thalami, and insula (Fig. 15-2A).

35
00:04:15,762 --> 00:04:21,637
The cerebral hemispheres should appear symmetric, and the cerebellum should not be visible.

36
00:04:21,637 --> 00:04:31,825
The biparietal diameter is measured perpendicular to the falx cerebri, from the outer edge of the skull in the near field to the inner edge of the skull in the far field.

37
00:04:31,825 --> 00:04:37,062
The head circumference is measured by placing an ellipse around the outer edge of the skull.

38
00:04:37,062 --> 00:04:43,137
The head circumference may also be calculated by averaging the biparietal diameter and the

39
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:48,862
occipito-frontal diameter and multiplying by π. Figure 15-2.

40
00:04:48,812 --> 00:04:50,875
Normal biometry.

41
00:04:50,875 --> 00:04:58,675
A. Transthalamic view depicts measurement of the biparietal diameter (BPD) and head circumference (HC).

42
00:04:58,675 --> 00:05:05,925
Landmarks include the cavum septum pellucidum (CSP), thalami (T), and insula (I).

43
00:05:05,925 --> 00:05:17,250
B. Abdominal circumference view (AC) shows measurement and landmarks, which include the stomach (S) and the confluence of the umbilical vein (U) and the left portal vein.

44
00:05:17,250 --> 00:05:20,512
C. Femur length measurement (FL).

45
00:05:20,512 --> 00:05:29,750
The abdominal circumference is measured in a transverse image that includes the stomach and the J-shaped confluence of the umbilical vein with the portal sinus.

46
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:35,225
An ellipse is placed just outside the fetal skin edge (Fig. 152B).

47
00:05:35,225 --> 00:05:41,575
The image should appear as round as possible and ideally contain no more than 1 rib on either side.

48
00:05:41,575 --> 00:05:50,937
The spine should be visible in cross-section at the 3 o’clock or 9 o’clock position, whereas the kidneys should not be visible, as they are lower in the abdomen.

49
00:05:50,937 --> 00:05:56,500
Abdominal circumference is the biometric parameter most affected by fetal growth.

50
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:02,912
An abdominal circumference below the 10th percentile may be used to diagnose fetalgrowth restriction .

51
00:06:02,912 --> 00:06:08,975
The femur length is measured with the ultrasound beam perpendicular to the long axis of the shaft.

52
00:06:08,975 --> 00:06:15,100
Calipers are placed at each end of the calcified diaphysis (Fig. 15-2C).

53
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:23,362
Throughout the second and third trimesters, the femur length to abdominal circumference ratio is normally 20 to 24 percent.

54
00:06:23,362 --> 00:06:33,387
If this ratio is below 18 percent, a skeletal dysplasia should be considered, particularly if other long-bone measurements are lagging (p. 302).

55
00:06:33,387 --> 00:06:42,512
As discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 340), a mildly foreshortened femur length measurement is also a minor marker for Down syndrome .

56
00:06:42,512 --> 00:06:58,500
Various nomograms exist for other fetal structures, including the transverse cerebellar diameter, ocular distances, nasal bone, ear length, jaw index, thoracic circumference, and lengths of the liver, kidneys, long bones, and feet.

57
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:10,500
They may be used to address specific questions regarding organ system abnormalities, congenital infection, or genetic syndromes (Appendix, pp. 1238–1241).

58
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:12,387
Brain And Spine.

59
00:07:12,387 --> 00:07:19,162
Standard sonographic evaluation of the fetal brain includes three transverse (axial) views.

60
00:07:19,162 --> 00:07:30,787
As noted, the transthalamic view should contain the midline falx cerebri, cavum septum pellucidum (CSP), thalami, and insula (see Fig. 15-2A).

61
00:07:30,787 --> 00:07:37,212
The CSP is the space between the two laminae that separate the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles.

62
00:07:37,212 --> 00:07:46,750
It should be visible between approximately 17 and 37 weeks’ gestation, but after this, fusion of the septi pellucidi may obliterate the cavum.

63
00:07:46,750 --> 00:07:52,700
Inability to visualize a normal CSP may indicate a midline brain abnormality .

64
00:07:52,700 --> 00:08:07,575
For example, the frontal horns are widely spaced apart in agenesis of the corpus callosum (ACC), whereas in cases of septo-optic dysplasia (de Morsier syndrome) and lobar holoprosencephaly, the frontal horns communicate.

65
00:08:07,575 --> 00:08:15,712
Discussed in Chapter 16 (p. 312), an abnormally wide CSP may also be found with trisomy 18.

66
00:08:15,712 --> 00:08:18,112
Figure 15-3.

67
00:08:18,112 --> 00:08:29,475
Absence of the cavum septum pellucidum, with coronal (A) and transverse (B) images showing communication between the frontal horns (FH) of the lateral ventricles.

68
00:08:29,475 --> 00:08:36,112
This may be isolated but can occur in the setting of septo-optic dysplasia or lobar holoprosencephaly.

69
00:08:36,112 --> 00:08:40,725
C = choroid plexus; F = falx cerebri.

70
00:08:40,725 --> 00:08:49,162
The transventricular view lies superior to the transthalamic view and, as the name implies, includes the lateral ventricles.

71
00:08:49,162 --> 00:08:55,350
The ventricles are measured at their atrium, which is the confluence of the temporal and occipital horns .

72
00:08:55,350 --> 00:09:01,187
The measurement is normally 5 to 9 mm throughout the second and third trimesters.

73
00:09:01,187 --> 00:09:06,375
Cerebrospinal fluid is produced within the ventricles by the choroid plexus.

74
00:09:06,375 --> 00:09:20,225
Choroid plexus cysts are present in 0.5 to 2 percent of uncomplicated pregnancies and approximately 30 to 50 percent of pregnancies with trisomy 18 (Fig. 16-5, p. 312) .

75
00:09:20,225 --> 00:09:25,012
A detailed ultrasound examination is generally offered when found.

76
00:09:25,012 --> 00:09:35,050
In the absence of an associated ultrasound abnormality, or unless an aneuploidy screening test indicates increased risk for trisomy 18, a choroid

77
00:09:35,212 --> 00:09:38,587
plexus cyst is considered a normal variant.

78
00:09:38,537 --> 00:09:41,012
Figure 15-4.

79
00:09:41,012 --> 00:09:43,299
Transventricular view.

80
00:09:43,299 --> 00:09:49,149
A. Transverse image of the lateral ventricles, which contain the choroid plexus (C).

81
00:09:49,149 --> 00:09:56,024
B. The ventricles are measured at the atria (A), the confluence of the temporal and occipital horns.

82
00:09:56,024 --> 00:10:01,812
The measurement is normally 5–9 mm. Vp = lateral ventricle.

83
00:10:01,812 --> 00:10:06,124
Reproduced with permission from Rosa Robles, RDMS.

84
00:10:06,124 --> 00:10:11,999
For the transcerebellar view, the transducer is angled back through the posterior fossa .

85
00:10:11,999 --> 00:10:21,449
Structures visible in this image include the midline falx cerebri, cavum septum pellucidum, thalami, cerebellum, and cisterna magna.

86
00:10:21,449 --> 00:10:30,537
The cerebellum and cisterna magna are measured, and between 15 and 20 weeks’ gestation, the nuchal skinfold thickness also is measured.

87
00:10:30,537 --> 00:10:38,987
From 15 until 22 weeks, the cerebellar diameter in millimeters is roughly equivalent to the gestational age in weeks .

88
00:10:38,987 --> 00:10:47,474
Cerebellar hypoplasia has been associated with various central nervous system (CNS) and non-CNS abnormalities .

89
00:10:47,474 --> 00:10:57,024
The cisterna magna should measure between 2 and 10 mm throughout the second trimester and may reach 12 mm in the latter part of the third trimester.

90
00:10:57,024 --> 00:11:02,987
It becomes effaced when the Chiari II malformation is present (p. 277).

91
00:11:02,987 --> 00:11:21,037
If the cisterna magna is enlarged, the differential diagnosis includes absence of all or part of the vermis (p. 279), a cyst such as an arachnoid cyst within the posterior fossa, or mega-cisterna magna, which is a diagnosis of exclusion and has an excellent prognosis.

92
00:11:21,037 --> 00:11:30,624
An increased nuchal skinfold measurement is associated with increased risk for Down syndrome, other genetic syndromes, and structural abnormalities .

93
00:11:30,624 --> 00:11:33,174
Figure 15-5.

94
00:11:33,174 --> 00:11:35,362
Transcerebellar view.

95
00:11:35,362 --> 00:11:45,599
A. Transverse image of the posterior fossa showing measurement of the cerebellum (Cereb), cisterna magna (CM), and nuchal skinfold thickness (NF).

96
00:11:45,599 --> 00:11:52,774
B. Third-trimester image depicting the cerebellar hemispheres (CH) and cerebellar vermis (V).

97
00:11:52,774 --> 00:11:57,037
The fourth ventricle (4) is anterior to the vermis.

98
00:11:57,037 --> 00:12:03,999
CSP = cavum septum pellucidum; I = insula; P = cerebral peduncles.

99
00:12:03,999 --> 00:12:11,062
Imaging of the spine includes evaluation of the cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral regions .

100
00:12:11,062 --> 00:12:16,174
Representative images are often obtained in the sagittal or coronal plane.

101
00:12:16,174 --> 00:12:22,999
However, imaging of each spinal segment in the transverse plane is more sensitive for anomaly detection.

102
00:12:22,999 --> 00:12:27,324
Transverse images demonstrate three ossification centers.

103
00:12:27,324 --> 00:12:37,137
The anterior ossification center is the vertebral body, and the posterior paired ossification centers represent the junction of vertebral laminae and pedicles.

104
00:12:37,137 --> 00:12:41,524
Ossification of the spine proceeds in a cranial-caudal fashion.

105
00:12:41,524 --> 00:12:53,874
The ossification of the upper sacrum (S1-S2) is not generally visible before 16 weeks’ gestation, and ossification of the entire sacrum may not be visible until 21 weeks .

106
00:12:53,874 --> 00:13:00,187
Thus, detection of some spinal abnormalities can be challenging in the early second trimester.

107
00:13:00,187 --> 00:13:02,699
Figure 15-6.

108
00:13:02,699 --> 00:13:04,874
Normal fetal spine.

109
00:13:04,874 --> 00:13:12,824
This sagittal image depicts the cervical (C), thoracic (T), lumbar (L), and sacral spine (S).

110
00:13:12,824 --> 00:13:21,512
Arrows denote the parallel rows of paired posterior ossification centers, which represent the junction of vertebral lamina and pedicles.

111
00:13:21,512 --> 00:13:24,287
# Neural-tube Defects.

112
00:13:24,287 --> 00:13:32,787
These defects include anencephaly, myelomeningocele (spina bifida), cephalocele, and rare spinal dysraphisms.

113
00:13:32,787 --> 00:13:38,974
Normally, the neural tube closes by the embryonic age of 26 to 28 days .

114
00:13:38,974 --> 00:13:53,649
The birth prevalence of neural-tube defects approximates 0.9 in 1000 in the United States and most of Europe and 1.3 in 1000 in the United Kingdom (Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

115
00:13:53,649 --> 00:13:58,812
Many neural-tube defects can be prevented with folic acid supplementation.

116
00:13:58,812 --> 00:14:09,549
When isolated, neural-tube defect inheritance is multifactorial, and the recurrence risk without periconceptional folic acid supplementation is 3 to 5 percent .

117
00:14:09,549 --> 00:14:20,137
Screening for neural-tube defects may be performed with either ultrasound alone or ultrasound in addition to maternal serum alphafetoprotein (MSAFP) level measurement .

118
00:14:20,137 --> 00:14:33,999
Between 15 and 20 weeks’ gestation, an upper MSAFP threshold of 2.5 multiples of the median (MoM) is anticipated to detect 95 percent with fetal anencephaly and 80 percent with myelomeningocele.

119
00:14:33,999 --> 00:14:35,549
However,

120
00:14:35,716 --> 00:14:44,216
detection with standard sonography is at least comparable to that with MSAFP (Dashe, 2006; Norem 2005).

121
00:14:44,166 --> 00:14:57,303
A detailed ultrasound examination is the preferred diagnostic test and may identify other abnormalities or conditions that also elevate MSAFP levels (Table 17-5, p. 338).

122
00:14:57,303 --> 00:15:03,966
Anencephaly is an absence of the cranium and telencephalon above the level of the skull base and orbits .

123
00:15:03,966 --> 00:15:12,403
Acrania is absence of the cranium with protrusion of disorganized brain tissue, and herniation of the latter tissue is exencephaly.

124
00:15:12,403 --> 00:15:21,128
Importantly, brain tissue visible in the late first trimester is often not seen when ultrasound is performed in the second or third trimester.

125
00:15:21,128 --> 00:15:25,541
Thus, anencephaly is the final stage of exencephaly.

126
00:15:25,541 --> 00:15:34,978
It is often diagnosed in the late first trimester, and with adequate visualization, virtually all cases may be diagnosed in the second trimester.

127
00:15:34,978 --> 00:15:37,466
Figure 15-7.

128
00:15:37,466 --> 00:15:40,216
Anencephaly/acrania.

129
00:15:40,216 --> 00:15:47,328
A. This transabdominal image at 11 weeks’ gestation depicts relatively subtle absence of the cranium.

130
00:15:47,328 --> 00:15:55,053
B. A transvaginal image at 11 weeks demonstrates more clearly the protrusion of a disorganized mass of brain tissue.

131
00:15:55,053 --> 00:15:59,666
C. By 14 weeks, this tissue resembles a “shower cap.

132
00:15:59,666 --> 00:16:02,866
CRL = crown–rump length.

133
00:16:02,866 --> 00:16:12,641
Sonographically, the cranial contour may appear abnormal in the first trimester and may resemble a “shower cap” in the late first or early second trimester.

134
00:16:12,641 --> 00:16:19,441
The face often appears triangular, and sagittal images demonstrate lack of an ossified cranium.

135
00:16:19,441 --> 00:16:24,478
Hydramnios from impaired fetal swallowing is common in the third trimester.

136
00:16:24,478 --> 00:16:27,578
Anencephaly is uniformly lethal.

137
00:16:27,578 --> 00:16:33,541
If the pregnancy is continued, perinatal palliative care consultation should be considered .

138
00:16:33,541 --> 00:16:41,153
Cephalocele is the herniation of meninges through a cranial defect, typically located in the midline occipital region .

139
00:16:41,153 --> 00:16:47,291
When brain tissue herniates through the skull defect, the anomaly is termed an encephalocele.

140
00:16:47,291 --> 00:16:54,191
Herniation of the cerebellum and other posterior fossa structures constitutes a Chiari III malformation.

141
00:16:54,191 --> 00:16:57,803
Microcephaly is common by the third trimester.

142
00:16:57,803 --> 00:17:07,703
Associated intracranial abnormalities are frequently visible, and survivors have a high incidence of neurological deficits and intellectual disability.

143
00:17:07,703 --> 00:17:16,616
Cephalocele is an important feature of the autosomal recessive Meckel-Gruber syndrome, which includes cystic renal dysplasia and polydactyly.

144
00:17:16,616 --> 00:17:23,241
A cephalocele not located in the occipital midline raises suspicion for amnionic-band sequence .

145
00:17:23,241 --> 00:17:25,603
Figure 15-8.

146
00:17:25,603 --> 00:17:27,653
Encephalocele.

147
00:17:27,653 --> 00:17:36,616
This transverse image depicts a large defect in the occipital region of the cranium (arrows) through which meninges and brain tissue have herniated.

148
00:17:36,616 --> 00:17:45,828
Spina bifida is characterized by defects in the vertebrae, typically the dorsal arches, and subsequent exposure of the meninges and nerve roots.

149
00:17:45,828 --> 00:17:53,316
The prevalence approximates 1 in 2000 births (Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

150
00:17:53,316 --> 00:18:00,466
Herniation of a meningeal sac and neural elements is a myelomeningocele (Fig. 15-9A).

151
00:18:00,466 --> 00:18:06,241
Less commonly, only an empty meningeal sac herniates, which is a meningocele.

152
00:18:06,241 --> 00:18:14,491
Transverse images are helpful to demonstrate separation or splaying of the lateral processes and characterize the level of the defect.

153
00:18:14,491 --> 00:18:21,066
Most cases are open spina bifida, which means that the defect includes skin and soft tissues.

154
00:18:21,066 --> 00:18:25,991
Closed defects are skin-covered and more challenging to detect prenatally.

155
00:18:25,991 --> 00:18:28,478
Figure 15-9.

156
00:18:28,478 --> 00:18:30,603
Myelomeningocele.

157
00:18:30,603 --> 00:18:34,716
A. Sagittal image of a lumbosacral myelomeningocele.

158
00:18:34,716 --> 00:18:39,466
Arrowheads indicate nerve roots within the anechoic herniated sac.

159
00:18:39,466 --> 00:18:43,741
The overlying skin abruptly stops at the defect (arrow).

160
00:18:43,741 --> 00:18:50,116
B. Transthalamic image demonstrating flattening of the frontal bones (arrows)—the lemon sign.

161
00:18:50,116 --> 00:18:59,466
C. Transcerebellar image depicting the banana sign, an anterior curvature of the cerebellum (arrows) and effacement of the cisterna magna.

162
00:18:59,466 --> 00:19:04,791
Detection of spina bifida is aided by two characteristic cranial findings .

163
00:19:04,791 --> 00:19:17,516
Flatting or scalloping of the frontal bones is termed the “lemon sign,” and anterior curvature of the cerebellum with effacement of the cisterna magna is the “banana sign” (Fig. 15-9B,C).

164
00:19:17,516 --> 00:19:23,266
These findings are manifestations of the Arnold-Chiari or Chiari II malformation.

165
00:19:23,266 --> 00:19:25,391
This develops when

166
00:19:25,540 --> 00:19:33,477
downward displacement of the spinal cord pulls a portion of the cerebellum through the foramen magnum and into the upper cervical canal.

167
00:19:33,427 --> 00:19:39,502
The biparietal diameter measurement often lags behind the other biometric parameters.

168
00:19:39,502 --> 00:19:52,940
Ventriculomegaly is common after mid-gestation, and 80 to 90 percent of infants with myelomeningocele require ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement (Adzick 2011, Chao, 2010).

169
00:19:52,940 --> 00:20:03,452
Affected children require multidisciplinary care to address problems related to the defect such as deficits in swallowing, bladder and bowel function, and ambulation.

170
00:20:03,452 --> 00:20:09,877
Fetal myelomeningocele surgery is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 372).

171
00:20:09,877 --> 00:20:12,502
# Ventriculomegaly.

172
00:20:12,502 --> 00:20:18,815
Distention of the lateral ventricles is considered a nonspecific marker of abnormal brain development .

173
00:20:18,815 --> 00:20:33,840
Mild ventriculomegaly is diagnosed when the atrial width measures 10 to 12 mm; moderate ventriculomegaly, when the measurement is 13 to 15 mm; and severe ventriculomegaly when >15 mm .

174
00:20:33,840 --> 00:20:38,402
Representative images are depicted in Figure 15-10.

175
00:20:38,402 --> 00:20:42,702
The choroid plexus may appear dangling in severe cases.

176
00:20:42,702 --> 00:20:45,165
Figure 15-10.

177
00:20:45,165 --> 00:20:47,352
Ventriculomegaly.

178
00:20:47,352 --> 00:20:50,077
A. Mild ventriculomegaly.

179
00:20:50,077 --> 00:20:53,140
The atria measured 11 mm.

180
00:20:53,140 --> 00:20:58,540
No associated abnormality or underlying etiology was identified.

181
00:20:58,540 --> 00:21:01,490
B. Severe ventriculomegaly.

182
00:21:01,490 --> 00:21:09,940
In this fetus with aqueductal stenosis, the atria measured 45 mm. Arrow denotes the dangling choroid plexus.

183
00:21:09,940 --> 00:21:20,077
Ventriculomegaly may occur secondary to a variety of central nervous system abnormalities and is associated with numerous genetic and infectious etiologies.

184
00:21:20,077 --> 00:21:34,140
Initial evaluation includes a detailed examination of fetal anatomy, amniocentesis for chromosomal microarray analysis, and testing for congenital infections such as cytomegalovirus and toxoplasmosis .

185
00:21:34,140 --> 00:21:46,652
Genetic syndromes resulting in ventriculomegaly include L1 Xlinked aqueductal stenosis, Joubert syndrome, Walker Warburg syndrome, hydrolethalus syndrome, and lissencephaly syndromes .

186
00:21:46,652 --> 00:21:51,940
Ventriculomegaly does not typically result in significant skull enlargement.

187
00:21:51,940 --> 00:22:03,527
Two exceptions—when macrocrania often occurs—are aqueductal stenosis and the asymmetric ventriculomegaly interhemispheric cyst callosal dysgenesis (AVID) syndrome .

188
00:22:03,527 --> 00:22:14,990
Fetal MR imaging should be considered to assess for associated abnormalities that may not be detectable sonographically (Herrera, 2020a; Katz, 2018) .

189
00:22:14,990 --> 00:22:20,152
Prognosis is determined by etiology, severity, and progression.

190
00:22:20,152 --> 00:22:34,090
In a systematic review of nearly 1500 mild to moderate cases, 1 to 2 percent were associated with congenital infection; 5 percent, with aneuploidy; and 12 percent, with neurological abnormality .

191
00:22:34,090 --> 00:22:41,527
With chromosomal microarray analysis, genetic abnormalities may be identified in 10 to 15 percent .

192
00:22:41,527 --> 00:22:53,165
The larger the atria, the greater the likelihood of an underlying CNS abnormality and subsequent abnormal outcome (Gaglioti, 2009; Joó, 2008).

193
00:22:53,165 --> 00:23:04,202
If ventriculomegaly is isolated and remains mild, development is normal in at least 90 percent, and if moderate, development is normal in at least 75 percent .

194
00:23:04,202 --> 00:23:09,727
Progression significantly raises the likelihood of abnormal neurological development.

195
00:23:09,727 --> 00:23:13,177
# Agenesis of the Corpus Callosum.

196
00:23:13,177 --> 00:23:19,715
The corpus callosum is a major fiber bundle that connects reciprocal regions of the cerebral hemispheres.

197
00:23:19,715 --> 00:23:26,440
It is best viewed in the midsagittal plane, and color Doppler may demonstrate the pericallosal artery .

198
00:23:26,440 --> 00:23:31,665
Agenesis of the corpus callosum has characteristic sonographic findings.

199
00:23:31,665 --> 00:23:38,327
The frontal horns are widely separated, and the occipital horns are rounded— which is called colpocephaly.

200
00:23:38,327 --> 00:23:42,827
Together these findings give the lateral ventricles a teardrop shape .

201
00:23:42,827 --> 00:23:48,402
A normal cavum septum pellucidum is not visible because of frontal horn displacement.

202
00:23:48,402 --> 00:23:54,477
In the midline, bundles of Probst represent fiber tracts that no longer cross in the midline.

203
00:23:54,477 --> 00:24:05,152
Ventriculomegaly is not uncommon, and without the corpus callosum bordering the third ventricle superiorly, the third ventricle may be elevated and mildly enlarged.

204
00:24:05,152 --> 00:24:07,715
Figure 15-11.

205
00:24:07,715 --> 00:24:10,065
Normal corpus callosum.

206
00:24:10,065 --> 00:24:14,552
A. Arrows point to the corpus callosum in this midsagittal image.

207
00:24:14,552 --> 00:24:19,340
B. Power Doppler image of the pericallosal artery (arrowheads).

208
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:21,852
Figure 15-12.

209
00:24:21,852 --> 00:24:24,715
Agenesis of the corpus callosum.

210
00:24:24,715 --> 00:24:26,515
A. This

211
00:24:26,668 --> 00:24:30,705
transverse image demonstrates a teardropshaped ventricle.

212
00:24:30,655 --> 00:24:39,768
The frontal horns (F) are widely separated, no cavum septum pellucidum is visible, and bundles of Probst (P) line the midline.

213
00:24:39,768 --> 00:24:49,993
B. There is mild ventriculomegaly, no cavum septum pellucidum is visible (arrow), and the third ventricle (3V) is elevated and enlarged.

214
00:24:49,993 --> 00:24:52,143
A = atria.

215
00:24:52,143 --> 00:25:06,193
In population-based series, agenesis of the corpus callosum occurs in 1 in 4000 to 5000 pregnancies (Ballardini, 2018; Stoll, 2019; Szabo, 2011).

216
00:25:06,193 --> 00:25:12,555
It is associated with other anomalies, aneuploidy, and more than 200 genetic syndromes.

217
00:25:12,555 --> 00:25:19,193
Thus, chromosomal microarray analysis should be offered, and genetic counseling can be challenging.

218
00:25:19,193 --> 00:25:28,618
In a review of apparently isolated cases, fetal MR imaging identified additional brain abnormalities in more than 20 percent of cases .

219
00:25:28,618 --> 00:25:45,405
If MR imaging does not identify associated abnormalities, normal developmental outcome in 67 to 75 percent of cases and severe disability in about 10 percent has been reported (des Portes, 2018; Sotiriadis, 2012).

220
00:25:45,405 --> 00:25:48,093
# Holoprosencephaly.

221
00:25:48,093 --> 00:25:56,655
During early normal brain development, the prosencephalon or forebrain divides as it becomes the telencephalon and diencephalon.

222
00:25:56,655 --> 00:26:06,805
With holoprosencephaly, the prosencephalon fails to divide completely into two separate cerebral hemispheres and underlying paired diencephalic structures.

223
00:26:06,805 --> 00:26:15,293
The most severe form, alobar holoprosencephaly, is characterized by a single monoventricle that surrounds fused thalami .

224
00:26:15,293 --> 00:26:20,230
In semilobar holoprosencephaly, hemispheres partially separate.

225
00:26:20,230 --> 00:26:29,155
Lobar holoprosencephaly refers to a variable degree of fusion of frontal structures and is more challenging to detect with prenatal ultrasound.

226
00:26:29,155 --> 00:26:35,980
The lobar form is among possible diagnoses when a normal cavum septum pellucidum cannot be visualized.

227
00:26:35,980 --> 00:26:52,618
Last, the middle interhemispheric variant of holoprosencephaly is characterized by communication between the midportion of the bodies of the lateral ventricles with separation of the frontal horns, such that the choroid plexus may prolapse from one lateral ventricle into the other.

228
00:26:52,618 --> 00:27:02,230
Differentiation into two cerebral hemispheres is induced by prechordal mesenchyme, which is also responsible for differentiation of the midline face.

229
00:27:02,230 --> 00:27:09,193
Craniofacial anomalies associated with holoprosencephaly are reviewed later (p. 283).

230
00:27:09,193 --> 00:27:11,918
Figure 15-13.

231
00:27:11,918 --> 00:27:14,568
Alobar holoprosencephaly.

232
00:27:14,568 --> 00:27:22,693
The thalami (Th) are fused and encircled by a monoventricle (V) with a covering mantle (M) of cortex.

233
00:27:22,693 --> 00:27:25,318
The midline falx is absent.

234
00:27:25,318 --> 00:27:29,768
Reproduced with permission from Rafael Levy, RDMS.

235
00:27:29,768 --> 00:27:34,618
The birth prevalence of holoprosencephaly is only 1 in 10,000.

236
00:27:34,618 --> 00:27:48,255
However, the abnormality has been identified in nearly 1 in 250 early abortuses, which attests to extremely high in-utero lethality (Orioli, 2010; Yamada, 2004).

237
00:27:48,255 --> 00:28:03,193
The alobar form accounts for 40 to 75 percent of cases, and 30 to 40 percent have a numerical chromosomal abnormality, particularly trisomy 13 (Orioli, 2010; Solomon, 2010).

238
00:28:03,193 --> 00:28:09,455
Conversely, of trisomy 13 cases, two thirds are found to have holoprosencephaly.

239
00:28:09,455 --> 00:28:12,468
# Dandy-Walker Malformation.

240
00:28:12,468 --> 00:28:23,480
This posterior fossa abnormality is characterized by agenesis of the cerebellar vermis, posterior fossa enlargement, and elevation of the tentorium cerebelli.

241
00:28:23,480 --> 00:28:33,068
The cerebellar hemispheres are visibly separated, and fluid in the cisterna magna communicates with the fourth ventricle through the cerebellar vermis defect .

242
00:28:33,068 --> 00:28:37,018
The birth prevalence approximates 1 in 12,000 .

243
00:28:37,018 --> 00:28:40,755
Associated anomalies and aneuploidy are common.

244
00:28:40,755 --> 00:28:53,155
These include ventriculomegaly in 30 to 40 percent, other anomalies in approximately 50 percent, and aneuploidy in 40 percent (Ecker, 2000; Long, 2006).

245
00:28:53,155 --> 00:29:05,368
Dandy-Walker malformation is also associated with numerous genetic and sporadic syndromes, congenital viral infections, and teratogen exposure, all of which greatly affect the prognosis.

246
00:29:05,368 --> 00:29:11,643
Thus, the initial evaluation mirrors that for ventriculomegaly (p. 278).

247
00:29:11,643 --> 00:29:14,305
Figure 15-14.

248
00:29:14,305 --> 00:29:16,768
Dandy-Walker malformation.

249
00:29:16,768 --> 00:29:28,005
A. The cerebellar hemispheres (CH) are widely separated by a fluid collection that connects the 4th ventricle (4V) to the enlarged cisterna magna (CM).

250
00:29:28,005 --> 00:29:32,918
B. Sagittal image depicts elevation of the tentorium (arrows).

251
00:29:33,076 --> 00:29:39,638
Inferior vermian agenesis is a term used when only the inferior portion of the vermis is absent.

252
00:29:39,588 --> 00:29:52,901
Even with partial and relatively subtle vermian agenesis, the prevalence of associated anomalies and aneuploidy is still high, and the prognosis is often poor (Ecker, 2000; Long, 2006).

253
00:29:52,901 --> 00:29:56,513
# Schizencephaly and Porencephaly.

254
00:29:56,513 --> 00:30:06,126
Schizencephaly is a rare brain abnormality characterized by clefts in one or both cerebral hemispheres, typically involving the perisylvian fissure.

255
00:30:06,126 --> 00:30:14,388
The cleft is lined by heterotopic gray matter and communicates with the ventricle, extending through the cortex to the pial surface .

256
00:30:14,388 --> 00:30:23,676
Schizencephaly is believed to be an abnormality of neuronal migration, which explains its typically delayed recognition until after midpregnancy .

257
00:30:23,676 --> 00:30:30,388
It is associated with absence of the cavum septum pellucidum, resulting in frontal horn communication.

258
00:30:30,388 --> 00:30:33,601
Ventriculomegaly is a common finding.

259
00:30:33,601 --> 00:30:36,238
Figure 15-15.

260
00:30:36,238 --> 00:30:38,351
Schizencephaly.

261
00:30:38,351 --> 00:30:44,826
This transverse image shows a large cleft extending from the right lateral ventricle through the cortex.

262
00:30:44,826 --> 00:30:49,801
Because the borders of the cleft are separate, the defect is termed open-lipped.

263
00:30:49,801 --> 00:30:54,151
Reproduced with permission from Michael Davidson, RDMS.

264
00:30:54,151 --> 00:31:03,326
In contrast, porencephaly is a cystic space within the brain that is lined by white matter and may or may not communicate with the ventricular system.

265
00:31:03,326 --> 00:31:06,926
It is generally considered to be a destructive lesion.

266
00:31:06,926 --> 00:31:20,763
Porencephaly may develop following intracranial hemorrhage in the setting of neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia or in an individual with a COL4A-1 mutation —a genetic condition which causes familial porencephaly.

267
00:31:20,763 --> 00:31:28,401
In a monochorionic twin gestation, acute hypotension following death of a cotwin also may create porencephaly.

268
00:31:28,401 --> 00:31:34,276
Fetal MR imaging should be considered when either of these CNS anomalies is identified.

269
00:31:34,276 --> 00:31:36,701
# Microcephaly.

270
00:31:36,701 --> 00:31:42,126
This condition indicates that the size of the head is profoundly smaller than expected.

271
00:31:42,126 --> 00:31:57,801
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016) recommends that fetal microcephaly be defined as a head circumference at least 3 standard deviations (SD) below the mean for gestational age (Appendix, p. 1237).

272
00:31:57,801 --> 00:32:11,626
However, many fetuses with measurements in this range have normal head size at birth, and therefore the diagnosis of pathologic microcephaly is not considered certain until the head circumference reaches 5 SD below the mean .

273
00:32:11,626 --> 00:32:14,401
The forehead is often upsloping.

274
00:32:14,401 --> 00:32:28,813
Microcephaly is associated with a wide range of underlying abnormalities, genetic syndromes, and congenital infections such as toxoplasmosis, rubella, and cytomegalovirus, herpes, or Zika infection .

275
00:32:28,813 --> 00:32:38,251
Findings that suggest infection include parenchymal and periventricular echogenic foci, ventriculomegaly, and cerebellar hypoplasia.

276
00:32:38,251 --> 00:32:43,551
Amniocentesis should be offered, and fetal MR imaging should be considered.

277
00:32:43,551 --> 00:32:55,851
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016) recommends performing a detailed ultrasound examination of the fetal brain if the head circumference measures more than 2 SD below the mean.

278
00:32:55,851 --> 00:33:06,938
Another ultrasound evaluation follows in 3 or 4 weeks, with the understanding that it may be difficult to distinguish constitutionally small head size from pathologic findings.

279
00:33:06,938 --> 00:33:10,226
# Sacrococcygeal Teratoma.

280
00:33:10,226 --> 00:33:21,951
This germ cell tumor is one of the most common tumors in neonates, with a birth prevalence of approximately 1 in 28,000 (Derikx, 2006; Swamy, 2008).

281
00:33:21,951 --> 00:33:28,176
It is thought to arise from the totipotent cells along the Hensen node, anterior to the coccyx.

282
00:33:28,176 --> 00:33:34,163
Sacrococcygeal teratoma (SCT) classification includes four types .

283
00:33:34,163 --> 00:33:51,226
Type 1 is predominantly external with a minimal presacral component; type 2 is predominantly external but with a significant intrapelvic component; type 3 is predominantly internal and has abdominal extension; and type 4 is entirely internal with no external component.

284
00:33:51,226 --> 00:33:56,301
The tumor histological type may be mature, immature, or malignant.

285
00:33:56,301 --> 00:34:06,576
Sonographically, SCT is a solid and/or cystic mass that arises from the anterior sacrum and usually extends inferiorly and externally as it grows .

286
00:34:06,576 --> 00:34:14,663
Solid components often vary in echogenicity, appear disorganized, and may enlarge rapidly with advancing gestation.

287
00:34:14,663 --> 00:34:17,176
Fetal MR imaging should

288
00:34:17,332 --> 00:34:22,607
be considered because internal pelvic components may be challenging to visualize.

289
00:34:22,557 --> 00:34:34,694
Large, solid tumors frequently result in hydrops due to high-output cardiac failure, either as a consequence of tumor vascularity or secondary to bleeding within the tumor and resultant anemia.

290
00:34:34,694 --> 00:34:37,019
Hydramnios is common.

291
00:34:37,019 --> 00:34:45,444
Fetuses with tumors >5 cm often require cesarean delivery, and classical hysterotomy may be needed .

292
00:34:45,444 --> 00:34:51,519
Fetal therapy for SCT is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 373).

293
00:34:51,519 --> 00:34:54,144
Figure 15-16.

294
00:34:54,144 --> 00:34:56,882
Sacrococcygeal teratoma.

295
00:34:56,882 --> 00:35:07,282
This tumor enlarged from 3 cm in diameter at 19 weeks’ gestation (A) to 9 cm in diameter during a 5-week period (B).

296
00:35:07,282 --> 00:35:10,782
Arrows depict the external borders of the mass.

297
00:35:10,782 --> 00:35:13,769
# Caudal Regression Sequence.

298
00:35:13,769 --> 00:35:20,782
This rare anomaly is characterized by absence of the sacral spine and often portions of the lumbar spine.

299
00:35:20,782 --> 00:35:26,969
It is approximately 25 times more prevalent in pregnancies complicated by diabetes .

300
00:35:26,969 --> 00:35:42,357
Caudal regression is associated with genitourinary malformations and syndromes such as the VACTERL association (vertebral defects, anal atresia, cardiac defects, tracheo-esophageal fistula, renal anomalies, and limb abnormalities) .

301
00:35:42,357 --> 00:35:52,982
Sonographic findings include a spine that appears abnormally short, lacks normal lumbosacral curvature, and terminates abruptly above the level of the iliac wings .

302
00:35:52,982 --> 00:36:00,444
Because the sacrum does not lie between the iliac wings, they are abnormally close together and may appear shield-like.

303
00:36:00,444 --> 00:36:06,207
The lower extremities lack normal soft tissue development and may be abnormally positioned.

304
00:36:06,207 --> 00:36:08,919
Figure 15-17.

305
00:36:08,919 --> 00:36:11,369
Caudal regression sequence.

306
00:36:11,369 --> 00:36:14,682
A. The spine is markedly foreshortened.

307
00:36:14,682 --> 00:36:17,507
The arrow shows where it terminates.

308
00:36:17,507 --> 00:36:22,582
B. The spine ends abruptly above the level of the iliac wings (I).

309
00:36:22,582 --> 00:36:28,619
C. Without a vertebral body between the iliac wings (I), they assume a shield shape.

310
00:36:28,619 --> 00:36:31,119
# Sirenomelia.

311
00:36:31,119 --> 00:36:36,694
This rare anomaly may be confused with caudal regression sequence but is quite different.

312
00:36:36,694 --> 00:36:45,332
Formerly termed mermaid syndrome, it is characterized by a single lower extremity in the midline and bilateral renal agenesis.

313
00:36:45,332 --> 00:36:49,519
The extremity may contain one or two sets of bones and feet .

314
00:36:49,519 --> 00:36:53,407
The bladder, anus, and genitalia are absent.

315
00:36:53,407 --> 00:36:59,882
What may appear to be a single umbilical artery in these cases is instead a remnant of the vitelline artery.

316
00:36:59,882 --> 00:37:10,332
After 18 weeks’ gestation, when the kidneys become the primary source of amnionic fluid production, resultant anhydramnios may complicate the diagnosis.

317
00:37:10,332 --> 00:37:18,244
Cases of surviving infants have a variant of sirenomelia in which there is some functioning renal tissue and urinary output .

318
00:37:18,244 --> 00:37:20,857
Figure 15-18.

319
00:37:20,857 --> 00:37:22,794
Sirenomelia.

320
00:37:22,794 --> 00:37:32,157
A. This single lower extremity contained two femur bones (F), two lower leg bones (LL), and a fused foot with toes pointing outward ().

321
00:37:32,157 --> 00:37:36,569
B. Arrows show the soft tissue outline of the lower extremity.

322
00:37:36,569 --> 00:37:42,557
Amnionic fluid is visible only because the gestational age is 17 weeks’ gestation.

323
00:37:42,557 --> 00:37:48,157
By 18 weeks, absence of kidneys and bladder resulted in anhydramnios.

324
00:37:48,157 --> 00:37:52,594
Reproduced with permission from Melissa Salvie, RDMS.

325
00:37:52,594 --> 00:37:54,919
# Hemivertebrae.

326
00:37:54,919 --> 00:38:02,082
These spinal segmentation-fusion defects are characterized by abnormal development of half of the vertebral body.

327
00:38:02,082 --> 00:38:08,107
Affected vertebrae are triangular and may be separate or fused to adjacent vertebral bodies.

328
00:38:08,107 --> 00:38:12,844
This leads to abnormal curvature of the spine such as scoliosis .

329
00:38:12,844 --> 00:38:17,907
Prenatally diagnosed cases typically involve the thoraco–lumbar spine.

330
00:38:17,907 --> 00:38:28,244
Associated abnormalities are common, particularly skeletal, renal, and cardiac defects (Basude, 2015; Yulia, 2020).

331
00:38:28,244 --> 00:38:34,494
Hemivertebrae are also a component of several syndromes, including the VACTERL association.

332
00:38:34,494 --> 00:38:37,207
Figure 15-19.

333
00:38:37,207 --> 00:38:42,382
Hemivertebrae result in abnormal spinal curvature in these coronal images.

334
00:38:42,382 --> 00:38:46,582
Reproduced with permission from Rose Muli, RDMS.

335
00:38:46,582 --> 00:38:49,157
Head, Face, And Neck.

336
00:38:49,157 --> 00:39:07,607
Craniofacial anatomy on the detailed anatomic survey may include cranial integrity and shape of the skull (Fig. 15-2A); images of the orbits, nose, and lips; views of the maxilla, mandible, hard palate, and tongue; images of the ears; and evaluation of the neck .

337
00:39:07,607 --> 00:39:12,969
Of these structures, only the upper lip is a component of the standard examination.

338
00:39:12,969 --> 00:39:18,457
At Parkland Hospital, we include an image of the sagittal profile as part of

339
00:39:18,628 --> 00:39:23,415
our standard examination to help detect micrognathia prenatally.

340
00:39:23,365 --> 00:39:25,890
Figure 15-20.

341
00:39:25,890 --> 00:39:29,053
Normal craniofacial and neck anatomy.

342
00:39:29,053 --> 00:39:33,628
A. Transverse images of orbits (O) and nose (N).

343
00:39:33,628 --> 00:39:37,203
The small circle within each orbit is the lens.

344
00:39:37,203 --> 00:39:42,103
The distance between the orbits roughly approximates the width of each orbit.

345
00:39:42,103 --> 00:39:50,628
B. Sagittal view of the face, depicting the nasal bone (NB), lips (L), maxilla (Max), and mandible (Man).

346
00:39:50,628 --> 00:39:54,190
C. Transverse image of the alveolar ridge.

347
00:39:54,190 --> 00:39:58,565
D. Coronal view of the nose, upper lip, and lower lip.

348
00:39:58,565 --> 00:40:01,365
E. Sagittal image of the neck.

349
00:40:01,365 --> 00:40:03,578
F. Image of the ear.

350
00:40:03,578 --> 00:40:08,015
Reproduced with permission from Devi Nanandhan, RDMS.

351
00:40:08,015 --> 00:40:14,990
When imaging the neck, particularly in the third trimester, identifying a nuchal cord is not uncommon .

352
00:40:14,990 --> 00:40:22,728
This finding is not associated with adverse outcome, and we do not alter fetal surveillance if a nuchal cord is detected.

353
00:40:22,728 --> 00:40:25,490
Figure 15-21.

354
00:40:25,490 --> 00:40:33,028
Nuchal cord incidentally noted with color Doppler in a transverse image of the fetal neck at 34 weeks’ gestation.

355
00:40:33,028 --> 00:40:36,528
# Dolichocephaly and Brachycephaly.

356
00:40:36,528 --> 00:40:40,015
The cephalic index reflects the skull shape.

357
00:40:40,015 --> 00:40:45,703
It is measured by dividing the biparietal diameter by the occipitofrontal diameter.

358
00:40:45,703 --> 00:40:50,053
The cephalic index is normally 70 to 86 percent.

359
00:40:50,053 --> 00:40:57,203
It is smaller if the head shape is flattened— dolichocephaly, and larger if the shape is rounded—brachycephaly.

360
00:40:57,203 --> 00:41:05,290
In such cases, the head circumference measurement more reliably estimates gestational age than does the biparietal diameter .

361
00:41:05,290 --> 00:41:12,028
These head shape variants may be normal or can be secondary to fetal position or oligohydramnios.

362
00:41:12,028 --> 00:41:19,528
Dolichocephaly can occur with neural-tube defects, and brachycephaly may be seen in fetuses with Down syndrome.

363
00:41:19,528 --> 00:41:28,140
A strawberryshaped skull describes a pattern of angulation typical of trisomy 18 (Fig. 165, p. 312).

364
00:41:28,140 --> 00:41:33,353
With any abnormal skull shape, craniosynostosis is a consideration.

365
00:41:33,353 --> 00:41:36,065
Figure 15-22.

366
00:41:36,065 --> 00:41:42,265
Transthalamic images demonstrating dolichocephaly (A) and brachycephaly (B).

367
00:41:42,265 --> 00:41:49,290
The biparietal diameter (BPD) and head circumference (HC) are measured in each image.

368
00:41:49,290 --> 00:41:52,715
# Abnormalities of Orbits and Nose.

369
00:41:52,715 --> 00:42:00,603
Subjectively, the distance in between the orbits is similar to the diameter of each orbit (see Fig. 15-20).

370
00:42:00,603 --> 00:42:03,065
The lens is often visible.

371
00:42:03,065 --> 00:42:11,640
Nomograms are available for the ocular diameter and for the interorbital and binocular distances (Appendix, p. 1240).

372
00:42:11,640 --> 00:42:15,765
Hypertelorism is a common finding in trisomy 18.

373
00:42:15,765 --> 00:42:22,378
Hypotelorism, with or without microphthalmia, may be found in the setting of holoprosencephaly.

374
00:42:22,378 --> 00:42:27,153
In its extreme form only a single orbit is visible—cyclopia.

375
00:42:27,153 --> 00:42:37,253
Other comorbid anomalies with holoprosencephaly include a single nostril—cebocephaly, or absence of the entire nose (arhinia) with proboscis .

376
00:42:37,253 --> 00:42:40,003
Figure 15-23.

377
00:42:40,003 --> 00:42:42,640
Abnormalities of the orbits.

378
00:42:42,640 --> 00:42:46,553
A. Hypertelorism in a fetus with trisomy 18.

379
00:42:46,553 --> 00:42:52,578
B. Hypotelorism in a fetus with trisomy 13 and alobar holoprosencephaly.

380
00:42:52,578 --> 00:42:55,028
C. Microphthalmia.

381
00:42:55,028 --> 00:42:58,440
This fetus also had trisomy 13.

382
00:42:58,440 --> 00:43:00,828
Arrows point to the eyes.

383
00:43:00,828 --> 00:43:03,590
Figure 15-24.

384
00:43:03,590 --> 00:43:07,853
Nasal abnormalities associated with holoprosencephaly.

385
00:43:07,853 --> 00:43:13,553
A. Sagittal profile depicting a proboscis (arrow) protruding from the forehead.

386
00:43:13,553 --> 00:43:19,940
B. Coronal image demonstrating the proboscis along with hypotelorism and absence of the nose.

387
00:43:19,940 --> 00:43:25,128
C. Coronal image demonstrating a single nostril (cebocephaly).

388
00:43:25,128 --> 00:43:28,628
D. Photograph of a newborn with cebocephaly.

389
00:43:28,628 --> 00:43:36,615
Nasal bone hypoplasia or sonographic absence is an aneuploidy marker that confers increased risk for fetal Down syndrome .

390
00:43:36,615 --> 00:43:39,578
It is not a structural abnormality.

391
00:43:39,578 --> 00:43:48,015
Nasal bone measurement is part of the detailed ultrasound examination and is measured only between 15 and 22 weeks’ gestation.

392
00:43:48,015 --> 00:43:56,403
The measurement is considered foreshortened if less than 2.5 mm or if more than 2 SD below the mean for gestational age .

393
00:43:56,403 --> 00:44:05,040
Hypoplasia of the nose is a different condition that can occur following warfarin exposure (Fig. 8-4, p. 156).

394
00:44:05,040 --> 00:44:07,853
Figure 15-25.

395
00:44:07,853 --> 00:44:10,703
Nasal bone (and its absence).

396
00:44:10,703 --> 00:44:16,715
A. Sagittal image of the profile showing measurement of a normal nasal bone at 19 weeks.

397
00:44:16,715 --> 00:44:22,953
B. Fetus with trisomy 21, also at 19 weeks, with no visible nasal bone.

398
00:44:22,953 --> 00:44:27,290
Reproduced with permission from Jason McWhirt, RDMS.

399
00:44:27,290 --> 00:44:29,728
# Facial Clefts.

400
00:44:29,728 --> 00:44:32,678
These are grouped into three main types.

401
00:44:32,678 --> 00:44:36,565
The first type, cleft lip and palate, always

402
00:44:36,724 --> 00:44:49,761
involves the lip, may also involve the hard palate, can be unilateral or bilateral, and has a birth prevalence that approximates 1 in 1000 (Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

403
00:44:49,711 --> 00:44:58,199
If isolated, the inheritance is multifactorial—with a recurrence risk of 3 to 5 percent for one prior affected child.

404
00:44:58,199 --> 00:45:07,674
If a cleft is visible in the upper lip, a transverse image at the level of the alveolar ridge may demonstrate that the defect also involves the primary palate .

405
00:45:07,674 --> 00:45:10,524
Figure 15-26.

406
00:45:10,524 --> 00:45:12,949
Cleft lip/palate.

407
00:45:12,949 --> 00:45:18,124
A. This fetus has a prominent unilateral (left-sided) cleft lip.

408
00:45:18,124 --> 00:45:24,749
B. Transverse view of the palate in the same fetus demonstrates a defect in the alveolar ridge (arrow).

409
00:45:24,749 --> 00:45:27,899
The tongue (T) is also visible.

410
00:45:27,899 --> 00:45:35,449
In one review of low-risk pregnancies, cleft lip was identified sonographically in only about half of cases .

411
00:45:35,449 --> 00:45:47,499
Approximately 40 percent of those detected prenatally are associated with other anomalies or syndromes, and aneuploidy is common (Maarse, 2011; Offerdal, 2008).

412
00:45:47,499 --> 00:45:53,361
The rate of associated anomalies is highest for bilateral defects that involve the palate.

413
00:45:53,361 --> 00:46:06,436
In the Utah Birth Defect Network, aneuploidy was identified in 1 percent with cleft lip alone, 5 percent with unilateral cleft lip and palate, and 13 percent with bilateral cleft lip and palate .

414
00:46:06,436 --> 00:46:10,324
The second type of cleft is isolated cleft palate.

415
00:46:10,324 --> 00:46:18,161
It begins at the uvula, may involve the soft palate, and occasionally involves the hard palate—but does not involve the lip.

416
00:46:18,161 --> 00:46:21,911
The birth prevalence approximates 1 in 2000 .

417
00:46:21,911 --> 00:46:37,024
Identification of an isolated cleft palate has been described using detailed sonography, and particularly with three-dimensional imaging, but detection is not feasible in all cases (Ramos, 2010; Wilhelm, 2010).

418
00:46:37,024 --> 00:46:47,236
Isolated cleft palate is not expected to be visualized during a standard ultrasound examination (Maarse, 2011; Offerdal, 2008).

419
00:46:47,236 --> 00:46:56,624
A third type of cleft is median cleft lip, which may be found in fetuses with holoprosencephaly, typically when hypotelorism is also present.

420
00:46:56,624 --> 00:47:05,686
Additionally, a median cleft may be associated with hypertelorism and frontonasal dysplasia, formerly called the median cleft face syndrome.

421
00:47:05,686 --> 00:47:08,136
# Micrognathia.

422
00:47:08,136 --> 00:47:18,874
The fetal profile can help identify cases of micrognathia—hypoplasia of the mandible, and retrognathia, which is recession of the mandible in relation to the maxilla .

423
00:47:18,874 --> 00:47:32,224
To quantify micrognathia risk, a transverse image of the mandible can be used to calculate the jaw index, which is the anteriorposterior diameter of the mandible expressed as a percentage of the biparietal diameter .

424
00:47:32,224 --> 00:47:43,724
Fetuses with micrognathia and retrognathia frequently have a posterior cleft palate and glossoptosis (recessed tongue)—a constellation of findings known as the Pierre Robin sequence.

425
00:47:43,724 --> 00:47:55,649
Micrognathia is also a feature of Treacher Collins syndrome, oralfacial-digital syndromes, trisomies 18 and 13, triploidy, and the 22 q11.2 deletion.

426
00:47:55,649 --> 00:48:01,199
Micrognathia may result in hydramnios and can cause airway obstruction at birth.

427
00:48:01,199 --> 00:48:10,999
Use of the ex-utero intrapartum treatment (EXIT) procedure for severe micrognathia is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 379).

428
00:48:10,999 --> 00:48:13,849
Figure 15-27.

429
00:48:13,849 --> 00:48:15,786
Micrognathia.

430
00:48:15,786 --> 00:48:19,911
A. Sagittal image of a fetus with severe micrognathia.

431
00:48:19,911 --> 00:48:26,436
B. 3-dimensional ultrasound rendering depicts the recessed chin and downslanting palpebral fissures.

432
00:48:26,436 --> 00:48:32,511
C. A transverse image of the mandible was used to calculate a jaw index for this fetus.

433
00:48:32,511 --> 00:48:41,861
Micrognathia should not be confused with agnathia–otocephaly, a rare anomaly in which no mandible develops and the ears may fuse in the midline .

434
00:48:41,861 --> 00:48:48,761
The latter confers an extremely poor prognosis and has been diagnosed as early as the late first trimester .

435
00:48:48,761 --> 00:48:51,474
Figure 15-28.

436
00:48:51,474 --> 00:48:57,386
Agnathia-otocephaly, ultrasound (A) and postdelivery (B) images.

437
00:48:57,386 --> 00:49:05,711
With this rare, lethal anomaly the mandible fails to develop, and the ears are inferiorly displaced and may be fused in the midline.

438
00:49:05,711 --> 00:49:08,036
# Epignathus.

439
00:49:08,036 --> 00:49:12,649
This rare teratoma arises from the oral cavity or pharynx.

440
00:49:12,649 --> 00:49:19,774
It may grow outward or both outward and into the brain, the latter conferring an extremely poor prognosis .

441
00:49:19,774 --> 00:49:29,274
If brain involvement is absent, an EXIT procedure, reviewed in Chapter 19 (p. 379), can help secure the airway at delivery .

442
00:49:29,274 --> 00:49:32,024
Figure 15-29.

443
00:49:32,188 --> 00:49:37,538
Epignathus, ultrasound (A) and postdelivery (B) images.

444
00:49:37,488 --> 00:49:47,338
This teratoma arises from the oral cavity or pharynx and may grow outward from the mouth or both outward and into the brain, as in this fetus (arrowhead).

445
00:49:47,338 --> 00:49:50,863
Arrows depict the external extent of the mass.

446
00:49:50,863 --> 00:49:55,575
Reproduced with permission from Halima Abdirahman, RDMS.

447
00:49:55,575 --> 00:49:58,188
# Cystic Hygroma.

448
00:49:58,188 --> 00:50:05,350
This venolymphatic malformation is characterized by fluid-filled sacs that extend from the posterior neck .

449
00:50:05,350 --> 00:50:11,150
Cystic hygromas may be diagnosed in the first trimester and vary widely in size.

450
00:50:11,150 --> 00:50:18,788
Impaired lymphatic drainage from the head into the jugular vein leads to an accumulation of fluid in jugular lymphatic sacs.

451
00:50:18,788 --> 00:50:23,763
The birth prevalence of cystic hygromas approximates 1 in 5000.

452
00:50:23,763 --> 00:50:31,688
However, reflecting the high in-utero lethality of the condition, the first-trimester incidence exceeds 1 in 300 .

453
00:50:31,688 --> 00:50:34,113
Figure 15-30.

454
00:50:34,113 --> 00:50:36,325
Cystic hygromas.

455
00:50:36,325 --> 00:50:42,988
A. This 9-week fetus with a cystic hygroma (arrow) was later found to have Noonan syndrome.

456
00:50:42,988 --> 00:50:50,813
B. Massive multiseptated hygromas (arrowheads) in the setting of hydrops fetalis at 15 weeks’ gestation.

457
00:50:50,813 --> 00:50:55,838
Up to 70 percent of cystic hygromas are associated with aneuploidy.

458
00:50:55,838 --> 00:51:09,600
When cystic hygromas are diagnosed in the first trimester, trisomy 21 is the most common aneuploidy, followed by 45,X and trisomy 18 (Kharrat, 2006; Malone, 2005).

459
00:51:09,600 --> 00:51:18,475
First-trimester fetuses with cystic hygromas are five times more likely to be aneuploid than fetuses with a thickened nuchal translucency.

460
00:51:18,475 --> 00:51:32,488
When cystic hygromas are diagnosed in the second trimester, approximately 75 percent of aneuploid cases are 45,X—Turner syndrome (Johnson, 1993; Shulman, 1992).

461
00:51:32,488 --> 00:51:43,100
Even in the absence of aneuploidy, cystic hygromas confer a significantly greater risk for other abnormalities, particularly flow-related cardiac defects.

462
00:51:43,100 --> 00:51:49,525
These include hypoplastic left heart and coarctation of the aorta (p. 292).

463
00:51:49,525 --> 00:52:07,288
Cystic hygromas may also be part of a genetic syndrome such as Noonan syndrome, an autosomal dominant disorder that shares several features with 45,X. Noonan syndrome is characterized by short stature, lymphedema, high-arched palate, and often pulmonary valve stenosis.

464
00:52:07,288 --> 00:52:15,538
Large cystic hygromas are usually associated with hydrops fetalis, rarely resolve, and carry a poor prognosis.

465
00:52:15,538 --> 00:52:25,988
Small hygromas may undergo spontaneous resolution, and provided that fetal karyotype and echocardiography results are normal, the prognosis may be good.

466
00:52:25,988 --> 00:52:39,063
The likelihood of a nonanomalous liveborn neonate with normal karyotype following identification of first-trimester hygroma approximates 1 in 6 (Kharrat, 2006; Malone, 2005).

467
00:52:39,063 --> 00:52:40,725
Thorax.

468
00:52:40,725 --> 00:52:47,863
Thoracic anatomy imaged in the detailed anatomic survey may include the lungs, ribs, and diaphragm.

469
00:52:47,863 --> 00:52:56,288
The lungs should appear homogeneous and symmetric, each occupying approximately one third of the area in the fourchamber view of the heart .

470
00:52:56,288 --> 00:53:02,888
The thoracic circumference is measured at the skin line in a transverse plane at the level of the fourchamber view.

471
00:53:02,888 --> 00:53:15,475
If pulmonary hypoplasia is suspected secondary to a small thorax, such as with a severe skeletal dysplasia, comparison with a reference table may be helpful (Appendix, p. 1238).

472
00:53:15,475 --> 00:53:21,788
Representative images of the diaphragm are usually obtained in the parasagittal or coronal plane.

473
00:53:21,788 --> 00:53:26,600
The diaphragm appears as a hypoechoic line between the lungs and liver.

474
00:53:26,600 --> 00:53:35,738
Thoracic abnormalities may appear sonographically as cystic or solid spaceoccupying lesions or as an effusion outlining the heart or lung(s).

475
00:53:35,738 --> 00:53:42,713
Fetal therapy for thoracic abnormalities is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 376).

476
00:53:42,713 --> 00:53:45,425
Figure 15-31.

477
00:53:45,425 --> 00:53:47,888
Normal thoracic anatomy.

478
00:53:47,888 --> 00:53:53,488
A. The lungs each occupy one third of the area in the fourchamber view of the heart.

479
00:53:53,488 --> 00:54:00,825
B. The diaphragm (arrow) appears as a hypoechoic line in between the lung and liver in this parasagittal view.

480
00:54:00,825 --> 00:54:09,150
LA = left atrium; LV = left ventricle; RA = right atrium; RV = right ventricle.

481
00:54:09,150 --> 00:54:12,038
# Diaphragmatic Hernia.

482
00:54:12,038 --> 00:54:17,613
This is a defect in the diaphragm through which abdominal organs herniate into the thorax.

483
00:54:17,613 --> 00:54:26,038
It is left-sided in approximately 75 percent of cases, rightsided in 20 percent, and bilateral in 5 percent .

484
00:54:26,038 --> 00:54:33,400
The prevalence of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) is 1 in 3000 to 5000 births

485
00:54:33,556 --> 00:54:37,918
Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

486
00:54:37,868 --> 00:54:46,756
Associated anomalies and aneuploidy are found in 40 percent of cases (Gallot, 2007; Stege, 2003).

487
00:54:46,756 --> 00:55:01,806
In population-based series, an associated abnormality reduces the overall survival rate of neonates with CDH from approximately 50 percent to about 20 percent (Colvin, 2005; Gallot, 2007).

488
00:55:01,806 --> 00:55:10,331
Without other abnormalities, the major causes of neonatal mortality are pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension.

489
00:55:10,331 --> 00:55:19,606
Left-sided CDH typically causes dextroposition of the heart to the right side of the thorax, such that the cardiac axis points toward the midline .

490
00:55:19,606 --> 00:55:30,256
Accompanying findings include the stomach bubble or bowel peristalsis in the chest and a wedge-shaped mass—the liver—located anteriorly in the left hemithorax.

491
00:55:30,256 --> 00:55:38,343
Liver herniation complicates at least 50 percent of cases and is associated with a 30-percent reduction in the survival rate .

492
00:55:38,343 --> 00:55:46,231
With large lesions, impaired swallowing and mediastinal shift may result in hydramnios and hydrops, respectively.

493
00:55:46,231 --> 00:55:48,918
Figure 15-32.

494
00:55:48,918 --> 00:55:51,793
Congenital diaphragmatic hernia.

495
00:55:51,793 --> 00:56:03,256
In this transverse view of the thorax, the heart is shifted to the right side of the chest by a left-sided diaphragmatic hernia containing stomach (S), liver (L), and bowel (B).

496
00:56:03,256 --> 00:56:19,418
An effort to reduce neonatal mortality rates and need for extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) has focused on prognostic indicators such as the sonographic lung-to-head ratio, described in Chapter 19 (p. 375).

497
00:56:19,418 --> 00:56:31,518
MR imaging parameters include measurements of lung volume and degree of liver herniation (Dutemeyer, 2020; Oluyomi-Obi, 2017; Worley, 2009).

498
00:56:31,518 --> 00:56:35,743
# Congenital Cystic Adenomatoid Malformation.

499
00:56:35,743 --> 00:56:43,606
This abnormality represents a hamartomatous overgrowth of terminal bronchioles that communicates with the tracheobronchial tree.

500
00:56:43,606 --> 00:56:59,518
It is also called congenital pulmonary airway malformation (CPAM), based on an understanding that not all histopathological types are cystic or adenomatoid (Azizkhan, 2008; Stocker, 1977 2002).

501
00:56:59,518 --> 00:57:14,593
The estimated prevalence is 1 in 6000 to 8000 births, and this rate is rising because of improved sonographic detection of milder cases (Burge, 2010; Duncombe, 2002; Lau, 2017).

502
00:57:14,593 --> 00:57:27,456
With ultrasound, a congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation (CCAM) is as a well-circumscribed mass that may appear solid and echogenic or may have one or multiple variably sized cysts .

503
00:57:27,456 --> 00:57:35,268
It usually involves one lobe, receives its blood supply from the pulmonary artery, and has pulmonary venous drainage.

504
00:57:35,268 --> 00:57:46,581
Lesions with cysts ≥5 mm are generally termed macrocystic, and lesions that appear solid or have cysts <5 mm are microcystic .

505
00:57:46,581 --> 00:57:49,331
Figure 15-33.

506
00:57:49,331 --> 00:57:53,968
Congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation (CCAM).

507
00:57:53,968 --> 00:58:06,506
A. This left-sided microcystic CCAM is an echogenic mass that fills the left hemithorax and causes mediastinal shift, which displaces the heart (arrows) to the right side of the chest.

508
00:58:06,506 --> 00:58:14,418
B. This left-sided macrocystic CCAM contains a cyst as large as the heart and also displaces the heart to the right.

509
00:58:14,418 --> 00:58:24,218
C. This right-sided CCAM contains multiple cysts of varying size () and displaces the heart farther to the left side of the chest (arrows).

510
00:58:24,218 --> 00:58:36,106
In a review of 645 CCAM cases, the neonatal survival rate exceeded 95 percent, and 30 percent of cases demonstrated apparent prenatal resolution.

511
00:58:36,106 --> 00:58:46,343
The other 5 percent of cases—typically very large lesions with associated mediastinal shift—were complicated by hydrops and had poor prognosis .

512
00:58:46,343 --> 00:58:58,518
Microcystic CCAMs usually become less conspicuous with advancing gestation, because in addition to occupying less of the thorax, their echogenicity more closely resembles surrounding lung tissue.

513
00:58:58,518 --> 00:59:05,556
However, a subset of CCAMs may demonstrate rapid growth between 18 and 26 weeks’ gestation.

514
00:59:05,556 --> 00:59:17,718
Corticosteroid therapy has been used for large microcystic lesions to forestall growth and potentially ameliorate hydrops (Curran, 2010; Peranteau, 2016).

515
00:59:17,718 --> 00:59:24,643
If a large dominant cyst is present, thoracoamnionic shunt placement may lead to hydrops resolution.

516
00:59:24,643 --> 00:59:30,456
Fetal therapy for CCAM is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 370).

517
00:59:30,456 --> 00:59:33,493
# Pulmonary Sequestration.

518
00:59:33,493 --> 00:59:39,806
Also called a bronchopulmonary sequestration, this abnormality is an accessory lung bud

519
00:59:39,964 --> 00:59:43,189
sequestered” from the tracheobronchial tree.

520
00:59:43,139 --> 00:59:45,914
It is nonfunctioning lung tissue.

521
00:59:45,914 --> 00:59:52,239
Most cases diagnosed prenatally are extralobar, which means they are enveloped in their own pleura.

522
00:59:52,239 --> 00:59:59,826
Overall, however, most sequestrations present in adulthood and are intralobar—within the pleura of another lobe.

523
00:59:59,826 --> 01:00:08,651
Extralobar pulmonary sequestration is considered significantly less common than CCAM, and no precise prevalence has been reported.

524
01:00:08,651 --> 01:00:14,101
Lesions have a left-sided predominance and most often involve the left lower lobe.

525
01:00:14,101 --> 01:00:19,476
Associated anomalies have been reported in approximately 10 percent of cases .

526
01:00:19,476 --> 01:00:28,639
Sonographically, pulmonary sequestration presents as a homogeneous, echogenic thoracic mass (Fig. 15-34A).

527
01:00:28,639 --> 01:00:32,639
Thus, it may resemble a microcystic CCAM.

528
01:00:32,639 --> 01:00:42,076
However, the blood supply is from the systemic circulation—from the aorta—rather than the pulmonary artery (Fig. 15-34B).

529
01:00:42,076 --> 01:00:46,726
Approximately 10 to 20 percent are located below the diaphragm.

530
01:00:46,726 --> 01:01:00,726
A small percentage of fetuses with pulmonary sequestration develop a large ipsilateral pleural effusion, and without treatment, this may result in pulmonary hypoplasia or hydrops (Fig. 15-34C).

531
01:01:00,726 --> 01:01:07,251
Treatment with thoracoamnionic shunting is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 376).

532
01:01:07,251 --> 01:01:15,414
Hydrops may also result from mediastinal shift or highoutput cardiac failure due to the left-to-right shunt imposed by the mass.

533
01:01:15,414 --> 01:01:25,714
In the absence of a pleural effusion, the reported survival rate exceeds 95 percent, and 40 percent of cases demonstrate apparent prenatal resolution .

534
01:01:25,714 --> 01:01:28,464
Figure 15-34.

535
01:01:28,464 --> 01:01:30,926
Pulmonary sequestration.

536
01:01:30,926 --> 01:01:39,426
A. Transverse image of the thorax depicts a left lower lobe pulmonary sequestration (PS) in this 25-week fetus.

537
01:01:39,426 --> 01:01:47,376
B. Sagittal image showing that blood supply to the mass is from a branch of the abdominal aorta, which confirms the diagnosis.

538
01:01:47,376 --> 01:01:58,939
C. Over the next 3 weeks, a large ipsilateral pleural effusion develops (asterisk), resulting in mediastinal shift and dextroposition of the heart to the far-right thorax.

539
01:01:58,939 --> 01:02:08,464
D. After placement of a double-pigtail shunt through the chest wall, which drains the effusion into the amnionic fluid, the lung significantly reexpanded.

540
01:02:08,464 --> 01:02:11,814
Arrows point to coils of the pigtail shunt.

541
01:02:11,814 --> 01:02:15,626
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Elaine Duryea.

542
01:02:15,626 --> 01:02:19,539
# Congenital High Airway Obstruction Sequence.

543
01:02:19,539 --> 01:02:24,551
This rare anomaly usually results from laryngeal or tracheal atresia.

544
01:02:24,551 --> 01:02:31,851
The normal egress of lung fluid is obstructed, and the tracheobronchial tree and lungs become massively distended.

545
01:02:31,851 --> 01:02:40,676
Sonographically, the lungs are brightly echogenic, the bronchi are dilated, the diaphragm is flattened or everted, and the heart is compressed .

546
01:02:40,676 --> 01:02:46,539
Impaired venous return leads to development of ascites, typically followed by hydrops.

547
01:02:46,539 --> 01:02:53,814
In one review of 118 cases, associated anomalies were identified in more than 50 percent .

548
01:02:53,814 --> 01:03:05,376
Congenital high airway obstruction sequence (CHAOS) is a feature of the autosomal recessive Fraser syndrome and has been associated with the 22q11.2 deletion syndrome.

549
01:03:05,376 --> 01:03:12,901
In some cases, the obstructed airway spontaneously perforates, which potentially confers a better prognosis.

550
01:03:12,901 --> 01:03:18,151
The EXIT procedure has significantly improved outcome in selected cases.

551
01:03:18,151 --> 01:03:20,976
Figure 15-35.

552
01:03:20,976 --> 01:03:25,151
Congenital high airway obstruction sequence (CHAOS).

553
01:03:25,151 --> 01:03:31,701
The lungs (L) appear brightly echogenic, and the bronchi (arrow) are dilated with fluid.

554
01:03:31,701 --> 01:03:37,176
Flattening and eversion of the diaphragm is common, as is ascites (asterisks).

555
01:03:37,176 --> 01:03:38,589
Heart.

556
01:03:38,589 --> 01:03:43,701
Cardiac malformations are the most common class of congenital anomalies.

557
01:03:43,701 --> 01:03:48,101
Their overall prevalence is 8 cases in 1000 births .

558
01:03:48,101 --> 01:04:05,539
Almost 90 percent of cardiac defects are multifactorial or polygenic in origin, another 1 to 2 percent result from a single-gene disorder or gene-deletion syndrome, and 1 to 2 percent may occur from exposure to a teratogen such as maternal diabetes or isotretinoin.

559
01:04:05,539 --> 01:04:19,576
Based on data from population-based registries, approximately 1 in 8 liveborn and stillborn neonates with a congenital heart defect has a chromosomal abnormality (Dolk, 2010; Hartman, 2011).

560
01:04:19,576 --> 01:04:30,676
Trisomy 21 accounts for most of these cases, followed by trisomy 18, 22q11.2 deletion, trisomy 13, and monosomy X .

561
01:04:30,676 --> 01:04:35,401
Approximately 50 to 70 percent of aneuploid fetuses with

562
01:04:35,572 --> 01:04:40,659
cardiac anomalies are also found to have noncardiac abnormalities.

563
01:04:40,609 --> 01:04:48,097
Traditionally, congenital cardiac anomalies have been more challenging to detect than anomalies of other organ systems.

564
01:04:48,097 --> 01:05:01,247
Recent series suggest that standard ultrasound may identify 50 to 60 percent of those with major cardiac anomalies before 22 weeks’ gestation (Byrne, 2020; Sun, 2018).

565
01:05:01,247 --> 01:05:19,272
Prenatal detection may improve neonatal survival, particularly for ductaldependent lesions, that is, those requiring prostaglandin infusion after birth to keep the ductus arteriosus open (Franklin, 2002; Mahle, 2001; Tworetzky, 2001).

566
01:05:19,272 --> 01:05:22,697
# Standard Cardiac Examination.

567
01:05:22,697 --> 01:05:40,809
Standard cardiac assessment includes a four-chamber view, evaluation of rate and rhythm, evaluation of the left and right ventricular outflow tracts, and when feasible, documentation of the 3-vessel view and 3-vessel trachea view (Figs. 15-36 and 15-37).

568
01:05:40,809 --> 01:05:55,309
It is hoped that examination of the cardiac outflow tracts and 3-vessel views will improve detection of outflow tract abnormalities that may have a normal-appearing four-chamber view, such as tetralogy of Fallot and transposition of the great arteries.

569
01:05:55,309 --> 01:06:06,947
Centers with expertise have reported at least 90 percent detection of such anomalies with incorporation of these views (Brandt, 2015; Palatnik, 2016).

570
01:06:06,947 --> 01:06:09,797
Figure 15-36.

571
01:06:09,797 --> 01:06:14,647
Diagram showing measurement of cardiac axis from the four-chamber view.

572
01:06:14,647 --> 01:06:22,972
LA = left atrium; LV = left ventricle; RA = right atrium; RV = right ventricle.

573
01:06:22,972 --> 01:06:25,809
Figure 15-37.

574
01:06:25,809 --> 01:06:29,759
Standard and detailed examination cardiac views.

575
01:06:29,759 --> 01:06:32,084
A. Four-chamber view.

576
01:06:32,084 --> 01:06:35,459
B. Left ventricular outflow tract view.

577
01:06:35,459 --> 01:06:40,334
Arrow depicts mitral valve becoming the posterior wall of the aorta.

578
01:06:40,334 --> 01:06:46,409
The arrow with asterisk marks the interventricular septum becoming the anterior aortic wall.

579
01:06:46,409 --> 01:06:49,984
C. Right ventricular outflow tract view.

580
01:06:49,984 --> 01:06:52,222
D. Three-vessel view.

581
01:06:52,222 --> 01:06:57,559
E. Three-vessel trachea view F. High short-axis view (outflow tracts).

582
01:06:57,559 --> 01:07:00,034
G. Aortic arch view.

583
01:07:00,034 --> 01:07:02,409
H. Ductal arch view.

584
01:07:02,409 --> 01:07:06,284
I. Superior and inferior vena cavae views.

585
01:07:06,284 --> 01:07:29,159
AA = aortic arch; Ao = aorta; DA = ductal arch; IVC = inferior vena cava; LA = left atrium; LV = left ventricle; PA = pulmonary artery; RA = right atrium; RV = right ventricle; SVC = superior vena cava; T = trachea.

586
01:07:29,159 --> 01:07:35,647
The four-chamber view is a transverse image of the fetal thorax at a level immediately above the diaphragm.

587
01:07:35,647 --> 01:07:51,584
It allows evaluation of cardiac size, position in the thorax, cardiac axis, atria and ventricles, foramen ovale, atrial septum primum, interventricular septum, and atrioventricular valves (Fig. 1537A).

588
01:07:51,584 --> 01:07:58,684
The left ventricle is apex-forming, and two pulmonary veins are often visible entering the left atrium.

589
01:07:58,684 --> 01:08:10,122
The atria and ventricles should be similar in size, and the apex of the heart should form a 45-degree angle with the left anterior chest wall (see Fig. 15-36).

590
01:08:10,122 --> 01:08:23,709
Abnormalities of cardiac axis are encountered in more than one third of fetuses with structural cardiac anomalies (Shipp, 1995; Crane, 1997; Sinkovskaya, 2015).

591
01:08:23,709 --> 01:08:31,109
The left ventricular outflow tract view demonstrates that the ascending aorta arises entirely from the left ventricle.

592
01:08:31,109 --> 01:08:43,459
The interventricular septum is shown to be in continuity with the anterior wall of the aorta, and the mitral valve in continuity with the posterior wall of the aorta (Fig. 15-37B).

593
01:08:43,459 --> 01:08:49,672
Ventricular septal defects and outflow tract abnormalities are often visible in this view.

594
01:08:49,672 --> 01:09:00,622
The right ventricular outflow tract view shows the right ventricle giving rise to the main pulmonary artery, which subsequently branches into the right and left pulmonary arteries.

595
01:09:00,622 --> 01:09:04,197
Fig. 15-37C,F).

596
01:09:04,197 --> 01:09:15,172
Together, the left and right outflow tract views demonstrate the normal perpendicular orientation of the aorta and pulmonary artery and the comparable size of these great arteries.

597
01:09:15,172 --> 01:09:21,522
The 3-vessel view (3VV) is a transverse image obtained just above the base of the heart.

598
01:09:21,522 --> 01:09:37,659
The three vessels line up in a row: an oblique view of the pulmonary artery, which should appear long and cylindrical; a crosssectional image of the ascending aorta; and a cross-sectional image of the superior vena cava (SVC) (Fig. 15-37D).

599
01:09:37,659 --> 01:09:41,884
The pulmonary artery and aorta should be similar in diameter.

600
01:09:41,884 --> 01:09:44,359
The 3-vessel trachea view

601
01:09:44,524 --> 01:09:49,936
3VTV) also is a transverse image but is obtained further cephalad.

602
01:09:49,886 --> 01:10:01,924
It includes the pulmonary artery giving rise to the ductus arteriosus as it makes a V-shape with the aortic arch, along with the SVC and the trachea (Fig. 15-37E).

603
01:10:01,924 --> 01:10:09,711
The 3VTV can be helpful for identifying aortic arch abnormalities, particularly ductaldependent lesions.

604
01:10:09,711 --> 01:10:13,336
# Specialized Cardiac Examination.

605
01:10:13,336 --> 01:10:27,874
The detailed ultrasound examination includes the five cardiac components of the standard examination plus the superior and inferior vena cavae and the aortic and ductal arch views (Fig. 15-37G–I).

606
01:10:27,874 --> 01:10:37,886
The examination also involves evaluation of the interventricular septum and cardiac situs, documentation of which may be facilitated with video clips.

607
01:10:37,886 --> 01:10:46,374
Fetal echocardiography is a more extensive examination of cardiac structure and function designed to characterize abnormalities.

608
01:10:46,374 --> 01:11:02,124
In addition to grayscale imaging views, echocardiography includes evaluation of cardiac rate and rhythm, color Doppler ultrasound, pulsed-wave Doppler ultrasound, cardiac biometry, and if clinically relevant, cardiac function assessment .

609
01:11:02,124 --> 01:11:05,824
These components are beyond the scope of this text.

610
01:11:05,824 --> 01:11:12,024
Echocardiography indications are discussed in Chapter 14 (p. 252).

611
01:11:12,024 --> 01:11:15,749
Selected cardiac anomalies are reviewed next.

612
01:11:15,749 --> 01:11:21,111
Ventricular Septal Defect This is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly.

613
01:11:21,111 --> 01:11:30,574
It is found in approximately 1 in 300 births (Bjornard, 2013; Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

614
01:11:30,574 --> 01:11:45,924
A defect may be appreciated in the perimembranous or muscular portion of the interventricular septum in the four-chamber view, and imaging of the left ventricular outflow tract may show discontinuity of the interventricular septum as it becomes the wall of the aorta .

615
01:11:45,924 --> 01:11:49,824
Color Doppler may demonstrate flow across the defect.

616
01:11:49,824 --> 01:12:02,799
Genetic abnormalities are diagnosed with chromosomal microarray analysis in approximately 1 percent of isolated cases but found in at least 15 percent if other structural abnormalities also are present .

617
01:12:02,799 --> 01:12:17,236
More than a third of prenatally diagnosed ventricular septal defects close in utero, and another third close in the first year of life (Cho, 2017; Paladini, 2002; Svirsky, 2019).

618
01:12:17,236 --> 01:12:20,011
Figure 15-39.

619
01:12:20,011 --> 01:12:22,686
Endocardial cushion defect.

620
01:12:22,686 --> 01:12:30,336
A. During ventricular systole, the lateral leaflets of the mitral and tricuspid valves come together in the midline.

621
01:12:30,336 --> 01:12:40,411
But the atrioventricular valve plane is abnormal, a common atrium (A) is observed, and there is a visible defect (arrow) in the interventricular septum.

622
01:12:40,411 --> 01:12:48,911
B. During diastolic filling, opening of the atrioventricular valves more clearly demonstrates the absence of their medial leaflets.

623
01:12:48,911 --> 01:12:57,049
Endocardial Cushion Defect This is also called an atrioventricular (AV) septal defect or AV canal defect.

624
01:12:57,049 --> 01:13:11,211
It has a birth prevalence of approximately 1 in 2500 and is associated with trisomy 21 in more than half of cases (Christensen, 2013; Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010).

625
01:13:11,211 --> 01:13:22,974
The endocardial cushions are the crux of the heart, and defects jointly involve the atrial septum primum, interventricular septum, and medial leaflets of the mitral and tricuspid valves .

626
01:13:22,974 --> 01:13:27,874
The majority of defects are balanced–with ventricles comparable in size.

627
01:13:27,874 --> 01:13:33,736
Some are unbalanced however, with one side of the heart significantly smaller than the other.

628
01:13:33,736 --> 01:13:45,236
The endocardial cushion defect is considered partial if there is absence of the atrial septum primum and a more subtle AV valve plane abnormality, with no ventricular septal defect.

629
01:13:45,236 --> 01:13:47,961
Figure 15-38.

630
01:13:47,961 --> 01:13:50,599
Ventricular septal defect.

631
01:13:50,599 --> 01:13:58,349
A. In this four-chamber view, a defect (arrow) is noted in the perimembranous portion of the interventricular septum.

632
01:13:58,349 --> 01:14:08,636
B. The left ventricular outflow tract view demonstrates a break in continuity (arrow) between the interventricular septum and the anterior wall of the aorta.

633
01:14:08,636 --> 01:14:16,861
Endocardial cushion defects are a common finding in heterotaxy, also known as cardiosplenic or isomerism syndromes.

634
01:14:16,861 --> 01:14:28,324
Heterotaxy implies that thoraco-abdominal organs normally on one side of the body are on an incorrect side, on both sides, or absent, as in polysplenia or asplenia.

635
01:14:28,324 --> 01:14:35,261
Complex cardiac abnormalities are a common feature, particularly endocardial cushion defects.

636
01:14:35,261 --> 01:14:38,199
In a review of 632

637
01:14:38,356 --> 01:14:45,093
pregnancies with fetal heterotaxy, an endocardial cushion defect was present in 60 percent .

638
01:14:45,043 --> 01:14:54,518
These endocardial cushion defects are a particularly at-risk group because of their association with third-degree heart block, which confers a poor prognosis.

639
01:14:54,518 --> 01:15:07,543
Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome This anomaly is found in approximately 1 in 4000 births (Bjornard, 2013; Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010;).

640
01:15:07,543 --> 01:15:15,731
During second-trimester ultrasound examination, the left ventricle may be so small that a chamber is difficult to appreciate .

641
01:15:15,731 --> 01:15:26,843
Alternately, the left ventricle may be normal in size or dilated but have severely decreased contractility and an echogenic inner wall due to endocardial fibroelastosis.

642
01:15:26,843 --> 01:15:36,543
There may be no visible left ventricular inflow or outflow, and reversal of flow may be documented in the aortic arch in the 3-vessel trachea view.

643
01:15:36,543 --> 01:15:42,043
As gestation advances, the left ventricle and aorta become progressively smaller.

644
01:15:42,043 --> 01:15:47,343
Prenatal detection is nearly 90 percent in population-based registries.

645
01:15:47,343 --> 01:15:49,843
Figure 15-40.

646
01:15:49,843 --> 01:15:52,631
Hypoplastic left heart syndrome.

647
01:15:52,631 --> 01:16:00,731
A. In this four-chamber view, the left ventricle appears “filled in” and echogenic, due to endocardial fibroelastosis.

648
01:16:00,731 --> 01:16:08,731
B. Color Doppler depicts only flow from the right atrium into the right ventricle, and no left ventricular filling is visible.

649
01:16:08,731 --> 01:16:17,293
C. Color Doppler shows reversal of flow in the aortic arch (arrow), which is perfused retrograde via the ductus arteriosus.

650
01:16:17,293 --> 01:16:25,618
LA = left atrium; LV = left ventricle; RA = right atrium; RV = right ventricle.

651
01:16:25,618 --> 01:16:33,881
Hypoplastic left heart syndrome is a ductal-dependent lesion for which neonatal administration of prostaglandin therapy is essential.

652
01:16:33,881 --> 01:16:42,168
Postnatal treatment consists of a three-staged palliative repair—single ventricle palliation, or cardiac transplantation.

653
01:16:42,168 --> 01:16:46,331
Rates of survival to adulthood may reach 70 percent .

654
01:16:46,331 --> 01:16:57,131
However, morbidity is high, and developmental delays are common (Lloyd, 2017; Paladini, 2017; Wilson, 2018).

655
01:16:57,131 --> 01:17:03,218
Fetal therapy for this condition is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 378).

656
01:17:03,218 --> 01:17:08,606
Coarctation of the Aorta Coarctation refers to narrowing of the aortic arch.

657
01:17:08,606 --> 01:17:12,668
The birth prevalence approximates 1 in 2500 .

658
01:17:12,668 --> 01:17:20,581
Usually, the narrowing is focal and found just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery at the aortic isthmus.

659
01:17:20,581 --> 01:17:25,018
Alternatively, a long segment of the aorta may be affected.

660
01:17:25,018 --> 01:17:37,381
Coarctation may be isolated or associated with other cardiac anomalies, such as hypoplastic left heart syndrome, unbalanced endocardial cushion defect, or double-outlet right ventricle.

661
01:17:37,381 --> 01:17:45,056
The most common sonographic finding is disproportion in ventricular size, with the left ventricle smaller than the right.

662
01:17:45,056 --> 01:17:53,768
However, only one third with this finding have coarctation (Ghi, 2018; van Nisselrooij, 2018).

663
01:17:53,768 --> 01:18:03,856
Other findings include narrowing of the aortic arch in the 3-vessel trachea view or narrowing of the isthmus in the aortic arch view– which may be challenging to image.

664
01:18:03,856 --> 01:18:13,406
Coarctation is associated with Turner syndrome (45,X), with the 22q11.2 deletion, and with autosomal trisomies.

665
01:18:13,406 --> 01:18:26,443
Ebstein Anomaly This rare anomaly is characterized by apical displacement of the tricuspid valve, such that the septal and posterior valve leaflets of the tricuspid valve attach closer to the cardiac apex .

666
01:18:26,443 --> 01:18:30,468
The birth prevalence is approximately 1 in 20,000 .

667
01:18:30,468 --> 01:18:36,206
Fetuses with Ebstein anomaly develop varying degrees of tricuspid regurgitation.

668
01:18:36,206 --> 01:18:44,093
In severe cases, right atrium becomes markedly dilated, and the fetus may develop cardiomegaly and hydrops.

669
01:18:44,093 --> 01:18:50,168
For many years, there was concern that lithium exposure predisposed to Ebstein anomaly.

670
01:18:50,168 --> 01:18:58,706
However, as discussed in Chapter 8 (p. 154), the absolute attributable risk is likely well below 1 percent.

671
01:18:58,706 --> 01:19:01,431
Figure 15-41.

672
01:19:01,431 --> 01:19:03,481
Ebstein anomaly.

673
01:19:03,481 --> 01:19:14,481
A. In this four-chamber view, the tricuspid valve’s septal leaflet attaches closer to the cardiac apex (arrowhead) than the corresponding mitral valve leaflet (arrow).

674
01:19:14,481 --> 01:19:19,393
B. The color Doppler blue jet reflects tricuspid regurgitation.

675
01:19:19,393 --> 01:19:27,718
LA = left atrium; LV = left ventricle; RA = right atrium; RV = right ventricle.

676
01:19:27,718 --> 01:19:39,181
Tetralogy of Fallot This anomaly occurs in approximately 1 in 3000 births (Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010; Nelson, 2016).

677
01:19:39,181 --> 01:19:41,168
It includes a

678
01:19:41,332 --> 01:19:51,107
ventricular septal defect; an overriding aorta; a pulmonary valve or pulmonary artery abnormality, and right ventricular hypertrophy .

679
01:19:51,057 --> 01:19:54,782
The last does not usually manifest before birth.

680
01:19:54,782 --> 01:20:00,907
Due to the location of the ventricular septal defect, the fourchamber view may appear normal.

681
01:20:00,907 --> 01:20:03,644
Figure 15-42.

682
01:20:03,644 --> 01:20:05,857
Tetralogy of Fallot.

683
01:20:05,857 --> 01:20:12,432
A. Left ventricular outflow tract view shows a ventricular septal defect with an overriding aorta.

684
01:20:12,432 --> 01:20:15,369
The arrow points to the aortic valve.

685
01:20:15,369 --> 01:20:21,594
B. Right ventricular outflow tract view demonstrating severe pulmonary artery stenosis.

686
01:20:21,594 --> 01:20:24,669
The arrow points to the pulmonary valve.

687
01:20:24,669 --> 01:20:32,844
C. In this fetus with tetralogy of Fallot with absent pulmonary valve, the pulmonary artery shows marked enlargement.

688
01:20:32,844 --> 01:20:41,294
Ao = aorta; LV = left ventricle; PA = pulmonary artery; RV = right ventricle.

689
01:20:41,294 --> 01:20:54,307
The pulmonary artery abnormality is pulmonary stenosis in 60 percent of cases, pulmonary atresia in slightly more than 25 percent, and absent pulmonary valve in 10 to 15 percent .

690
01:20:54,307 --> 01:21:01,619
Absence of the pulmonary valve leads to marked enlargement of the pulmonary artery and poses a risk for hydrops.

691
01:21:01,619 --> 01:21:07,432
The enlarged pulmonary artery can also compress the trachea and cause tracheomalacia.

692
01:21:07,432 --> 01:21:14,394
Chromosomal abnormalities are identified in approximately one third of fetuses with tetralogy of Fallot.

693
01:21:14,394 --> 01:21:23,294
Of these, 22q11.2 deletions compose 15 to 20 percent, and autosomal trisomies constitute 10 percent .

694
01:21:23,294 --> 01:21:31,244
D-Transposition of the Great Arteries This anomaly is characterized by outflow tracts that arise in parallel from the ventricles.

695
01:21:31,244 --> 01:21:37,257
The right ventricle gives rise to the aorta, and the left ventricle to the pulmonary artery .

696
01:21:37,257 --> 01:21:41,144
Thus, there is ventriculo-arterial discordance.

697
01:21:41,144 --> 01:21:44,919
The birth prevalence approximates 1 in 4000 .

698
01:21:44,919 --> 01:21:47,832
The four-chamber view is often normal.

699
01:21:47,832 --> 01:21:54,757
Prenatal detection approximates 40 percent but is thought to improve with visualization of the outflow tracts .

700
01:21:54,757 --> 01:22:02,869
Additionally, the 3-vessel views may demonstrate only two vessels, because the pulmonary artery is underneath the aorta.

701
01:22:02,869 --> 01:22:05,657
Figure 15-43.

702
01:22:05,657 --> 01:22:08,719
D-transposition of the great arteries.

703
01:22:08,719 --> 01:22:14,594
A. Transverse image with color Doppler depicting the outflow tracts arising in parallel.

704
01:22:14,594 --> 01:22:21,507
B. View of the left ventricle giving rise to the pulmonary artery, which subsequently branches (arrows).

705
01:22:21,507 --> 01:22:30,932
C. Sagittal image of the aorta arising anteriorly from the right ventricle and parallel to the pulmonary artery, which arises posteriorly.

706
01:22:30,932 --> 01:22:39,382
Ao = aorta; LV = left ventricle; PA = pulmonary artery; RV = right ventricle.

707
01:22:39,382 --> 01:22:50,519
D-transposition contrasts with L- or corrected transposition of the great arteries, in which there is atrio-ventricular discordance in addition to ventricular-arterial discordance.

708
01:22:50,519 --> 01:22:59,632
L-transposition is strongly associated with other cardiac anomalies and is much less likely to be diagnosed prenatally as an isolated finding.

709
01:22:59,632 --> 01:23:08,569
Double-outlet Right Ventricle With this anomaly, the majority of blood flow to both the pulmonary artery and the aorta comes from the right ventricle.

710
01:23:08,569 --> 01:23:15,169
Double-outlet right ventricle (DORV) is always associated with a ventricular septal defect.

711
01:23:15,169 --> 01:23:18,607
DORV has a spectrum of presentation.

712
01:23:18,607 --> 01:23:28,119
It is categorized according to the location of the ventricular septal defect and the relative proportion of blood flow from the right ventricle to the outflow tracts.

713
01:23:28,119 --> 01:23:32,769
The outflow tracts are often malposed, arising in parallel.

714
01:23:32,769 --> 01:23:36,707
The birth prevalence approximates 1 in 20,000 .

715
01:23:36,707 --> 01:23:48,969
Truncus Arteriosus This rare anomaly is characterized by a single, large outflow tract exiting the heart—a common arterial trunk—rather than a separate aorta and pulmonary artery.

716
01:23:48,969 --> 01:23:59,994
A prominent ventricular septal defect is usually identified, with an enlarged overriding outflow tract that gives rise to pulmonary arteries as well as head and neck vessels .

717
01:23:59,994 --> 01:24:05,457
The differential diagnosis includes tetralogy of Fallot with pulmonary atresia.

718
01:24:05,457 --> 01:24:11,082
The birth prevalence of truncus arteriosus approximates 1 in 16,000 .

719
01:24:11,082 --> 01:24:20,294
In 1949, Collett and Edwards categorized four types of truncus arteriosus according to the origin of the pulmonary arteries.

720
01:24:20,294 --> 01:24:25,644
Characterization of anatomic variants can often only be made postnatally.

721
01:24:25,644 --> 01:24:28,407
Figure 15-44.

722
01:24:28,407 --> 01:24:30,794
Truncus arteriosus.

723
01:24:30,794 --> 01:24:35,857
Grayscale (A) and color Doppler (B) images depict a

724
01:24:36,028 --> 01:24:38,865
single, large outflow tract.

725
01:24:38,815 --> 01:24:49,115
The common arterial trunk (CAT) overlies a ventricular septal defect (arrowhead) and gives rise to the head and neck vessels and pulmonary arteries.

726
01:24:49,115 --> 01:24:53,553
LV = left ventricle; RV = right ventricle.

727
01:24:53,553 --> 01:24:58,015
Reproduced with permission from Paul Mallamaci, RDMS.

728
01:24:58,015 --> 01:25:02,328
Cardiac Rhabdomyoma This is the most common cardiac tumor.

729
01:25:02,328 --> 01:25:12,003
Approximately 50 percent of cases are associated with tuberous sclerosis, an autosomal dominant disease with multiorgan system manifestations.

730
01:25:12,003 --> 01:25:20,115
Tuberous sclerosis is caused by mutations in the hamartin (TSC1) and tuberin (TSC2) genes.

731
01:25:20,115 --> 01:25:29,128
Sonographically, cardiac rhabdomyomas are well-circumscribed echogenic masses, usually within the ventricles or outflow tracts .

732
01:25:29,128 --> 01:25:37,203
They may be single or multiple, may grow in size during gestation, and may occasionally lead to inflow or outflow obstruction.

733
01:25:37,203 --> 01:25:48,328
In cases without obstruction or large tumor size, the prognosis is relatively good from a cardiac standpoint, because the tumors tend to regress after the neonatal period.

734
01:25:48,328 --> 01:26:00,965
Because extracardiac findings of tuberous sclerosis may not be apparent with prenatal sonography, MR imaging may be considered to evaluate fetal CNS anatomy (p. 266).

735
01:26:00,965 --> 01:26:03,778
Figure 15-45.

736
01:26:03,778 --> 01:26:05,628
Rhabdomyoma.

737
01:26:05,628 --> 01:26:15,465
In this four-chamber view of the heart, a large, echogenic, wellcircumscribed mass (R) fills the right ventricle and abuts the tricuspid valve (arrow).

738
01:26:15,465 --> 01:26:20,778
Despite its size, the mass did not obstruct flow, and the neonate did well.

739
01:26:20,778 --> 01:26:22,865
# M-Mode.

740
01:26:22,865 --> 01:26:31,365
Motion-mode or M-mode imaging is a linear display of cardiac cycle events, with time on the x-axis and motion on the y-axis.

741
01:26:31,365 --> 01:26:38,653
It is often used to measure embryonic or fetal heart rate (Fig. 14-1, p. 248).

742
01:26:38,653 --> 01:26:47,778
If an abnormality of heart rate or rhythm is identified, M-mode imaging permits separate evaluation of atrial and ventricular waveforms.

743
01:26:47,778 --> 01:26:53,865
Thus, it is particularly useful for characterizing arrhythmias and their response to treatment .

744
01:26:53,865 --> 01:27:00,215
M-mode can also be used to assess ventricular function and atrial and ventricular outputs.

745
01:27:00,215 --> 01:27:08,715
Premature Atrial Contractions Also called atrial extrasystoles, these are the most common fetal arrhythmia and a frequent finding.

746
01:27:08,715 --> 01:27:16,528
They represent cardiac conduction system immaturity and often resolve with advancing gestation or in the neonatal period.

747
01:27:16,528 --> 01:27:22,928
Premature atrial contractions (PACs) may be conducted and thus sound like extra beats.

748
01:27:22,928 --> 01:27:28,953
However, they are more commonly blocked, and with handheld Doppler they sound like dropped beats.

749
01:27:28,953 --> 01:27:39,153
As shown in Figure 15-46, the dropped beat may be demonstrated with M-mode evaluation as a compensatory pause that follows the premature contraction.

750
01:27:39,153 --> 01:27:42,015
Figure 15-46.

751
01:27:42,015 --> 01:27:43,540
M-mode.

752
01:27:43,540 --> 01:27:50,815
This image demonstrates normal concordance between atrial (A) and ventricular (V) contractions.

753
01:27:50,815 --> 01:27:55,203
Movement of the tricuspid valve (T) is also shown.

754
01:27:55,203 --> 01:28:01,115
The blue arrow denotes a premature atrial contraction followed by a compensatory pause.

755
01:28:01,115 --> 01:28:06,078
PACs are not associated with major structural cardiac abnormalities.

756
01:28:06,078 --> 01:28:16,578
Older case reports describe an association with maternal caffeine consumption and with hydralazine (Lodeiro, 1989; Oei, 1989).

757
01:28:16,578 --> 01:28:28,078
In approximately 2 percent of cases, affected fetuses are later identified to have supraventricular tachycardia (SVT), which is an arrhythmia that requires urgent treatment .

758
01:28:28,078 --> 01:28:37,415
Accordingly, pregnancies with fetal PACs are often followed with fetal heart rate assessment as often as every 1 to 2 weeks until the ectopy resolves.

759
01:28:37,415 --> 01:28:44,553
Treatment of fetal SVT and other arrhythmias is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 368).

760
01:28:44,553 --> 01:28:46,115
Abdomen.

761
01:28:46,115 --> 01:28:58,840
Abdominal anatomy visible in the second and third trimester includes the stomach, liver, gallbladder, spleen, adrenal glands, kidneys, renal arteries, small and large bowel, and ventral wall.

762
01:28:58,840 --> 01:29:03,890
The stomach is nearly always identified after 14 weeks’ gestation.

763
01:29:03,890 --> 01:29:18,828
Nonvisualization of the stomach may be secondary to impaired swallowing in the setting of oligohydramnios or to underlying causes such as esophageal atresia, a craniofacial anomaly, or a CNS or musculoskeletal abnormality.

764
01:29:18,828 --> 01:29:22,878
Fetuses with hydrops can also have impaired swallowing.

765
01:29:22,878 --> 01:29:30,128
Both the liver and spleen may be viewed in a transverse image obtained at the level of the stomach and intrahepatic portion of the

766
01:29:30,292 --> 01:29:37,129
umbilical vein—the plane at which the abdominal circumference is measured (see Fig. 15-2B).

767
01:29:37,079 --> 01:29:42,117
Hepatosplenomegaly may occur with congenital infection or with hydrops.

768
01:29:42,117 --> 01:29:51,154
By convention, the liver is measured in the sagittal or coronal plane, from the top of the right hemidiaphragm to the inferior tip of the right lobe .

769
01:29:51,154 --> 01:29:55,279
The spleen is posterior to the stomach in the transverse plane.

770
01:29:55,279 --> 01:30:01,479
The gallbladder may be imaged just inferior to the level at which the abdominal circumference is measured.

771
01:30:01,479 --> 01:30:10,679
It lies to the right of the intrahepatic portion of the umbilical vein and has a conical or teardrop shape (see Fig. 15-47B).

772
01:30:10,679 --> 01:30:13,517
Figure 15-47.

773
01:30:13,517 --> 01:30:15,692
Abdominal organs.

774
01:30:15,692 --> 01:30:23,242
A. The liver is measured from the top of the right hemidiaphragm to the inferior tip of the right lobe in this coronal image.

775
01:30:23,242 --> 01:30:37,417
B. Transverse image of the abdomen just inferior to the level at which the abdominal circumference is measured, depicting the gallbladder (G), stomach (S), spleen (Sp), liver (L), and right adrenal gland (A).

776
01:30:37,417 --> 01:30:41,217
The appearance of fetal bowel changes with maturation.

777
01:30:41,217 --> 01:30:51,217
Increased bowel echogenicity may indicate a small amount of swallowed intraamnionic blood, especially if the maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein level is elevated.

778
01:30:51,217 --> 01:30:57,892
The bowel appears as bright as bone in approximately 0.5 percent of secondtrimester fetuses.

779
01:30:57,892 --> 01:31:05,804
In such cases, the risk for fetal trisomy 21 is increased (Fig. 17-3, p. 340).

780
01:31:05,804 --> 01:31:16,204
Echogenic bowel is also associated with fetal cytomegalovirus infection and with cystic fibrosis, in which echogenicity represents inspissated meconium.

781
01:31:16,204 --> 01:31:19,404
# Gastrointestinal Obstruction.

782
01:31:19,404 --> 01:31:24,629
Bowel atresia is characterized by obstruction and proximal bowel dilation.

783
01:31:24,629 --> 01:31:30,854
In general, the more proximal the obstruction, the more likely it is to lead to hydramnios.

784
01:31:30,854 --> 01:31:42,392
The degree of hydramnios from proximal small-bowel obstruction can be sufficiently severe to result in maternal respiratory compromise or preterm labor and may necessitate amnioreduction .

785
01:31:42,392 --> 01:31:52,279
Esophageal Atresia The birth prevalence of this anomaly approximates 1 in 4000 (Cragan, 2009; Pedersen, 2012).

786
01:31:52,279 --> 01:31:57,792
It may be suspected when there is no visible stomach bubble or when the stomach is contracted.

787
01:31:57,792 --> 01:32:08,354
However, because esophageal atresia is associated with a tracheoesophageal fistula in up to 90 percent of cases, fluid is often able to enter the stomach.

788
01:32:08,354 --> 01:32:15,829
More than 50 percent of those with esophageal atresia have other abnormalities or underlying genetic syndromes.

789
01:32:15,829 --> 01:32:24,642
Multiple malformations are present in 30 percent of cases, and aneuploidy such as trisomy 18 or 21, in 10 percent.

790
01:32:24,642 --> 01:32:31,404
Approximately 10 percent of cases of esophageal atresia is found as part of the VACTERL association .

791
01:32:31,404 --> 01:32:40,642
Duodenal Atresia This anomaly occurs in approximately 1 in 10,000 births (Best, 2012; Dolk, 2010).

792
01:32:40,642 --> 01:32:48,704
It is characterized by the sonographic double-bubble sign, which develops from distention of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum .

793
01:32:48,704 --> 01:32:53,292
This finding may not be obvious before 24 weeks’ gestation.

794
01:32:53,292 --> 01:33:01,029
Demonstrating continuity between the stomach and proximal duodenum confirms that the second “bubble” is the proximal duodenum.

795
01:33:01,029 --> 01:33:10,917
Approximately 30 percent of affected fetuses have an associated chromosomal abnormality or genetic syndrome, particularly trisomy 21.

796
01:33:10,917 --> 01:33:21,379
Of cases without a genetic abnormality, a third have associated anomalies, most commonly cardiac defects and other gastrointestinal abnormalities .

797
01:33:21,379 --> 01:33:24,179
Figure 15-48.

798
01:33:24,179 --> 01:33:26,304
Duodenal atresia.

799
01:33:26,304 --> 01:33:34,454
Transverse image of the double-bubble sign, which represents distension of the stomach (S) and the first part of the duodenum (D).

800
01:33:34,454 --> 01:33:40,292
Continuity between these structures confirms that the second cystic structure is the duodenum.

801
01:33:40,292 --> 01:33:49,654
Jejunoileal Atresia This condition may present with dilated small-bowel loops that fill the abdomen or with meconium peritonitis from bowel perforation .

802
01:33:49,654 --> 01:33:59,642
Associated gastrointestinal abnormalities are identified postnatally in 25 percent of cases, with malrotation in 10 to 15 percent .

803
01:33:59,642 --> 01:34:04,604
Cystic fibrosis also is identified in approximately 10 percent.

804
01:34:04,604 --> 01:34:07,367
Figure 15-49.

805
01:34:07,367 --> 01:34:11,329
Jejunoileal atresia with meconium peritonitis.

806
01:34:11,329 --> 01:34:18,092
A. Sagittal image of the fetal abdomen at 26 weeks’ gestation with severe ascites following bowel

807
01:34:18,244 --> 01:34:20,044
perforation.

808
01:34:19,994 --> 01:34:25,119
Arrows point to the bowel, which is considerably more echogenic than the liver.

809
01:34:25,119 --> 01:34:30,219
B. Transverse image at 30 weeks demonstrates resolution of ascites.

810
01:34:30,219 --> 01:34:34,356
The abdomen is now filled with dilated loops of small bowel.

811
01:34:34,356 --> 01:34:39,269
Focal bright echoes (arrows) represent extraluminal meconium.

812
01:34:39,269 --> 01:34:42,494
A = ascites; L = liver.

813
01:34:42,494 --> 01:34:51,931
In general, jejunal atresia is more strongly associated with bowel dilation and hyperperistalsis, and ileal atresia, with perforation.

814
01:34:51,931 --> 01:35:00,706
Bowel dilatation in jejunal atresia typically does not present until after 24 weeks’ gestation and may be accompanied by hydramnios.

815
01:35:00,706 --> 01:35:09,744
Perforation is frequently associated with ascites, and bright echoes may be visible outside the bowel lumen, outlining the peritoneal cavity.

816
01:35:09,744 --> 01:35:15,919
Over time, the ascites resolves, and the extravasated meconium may form a pseudocyst.

817
01:35:15,919 --> 01:35:20,769
Fetal MR imaging can assist with identifying the level of the defect .

818
01:35:20,769 --> 01:35:27,631
Anal Atresia Also known as imperforate anus, this condition is less readily diagnosed by sonography.

819
01:35:27,631 --> 01:35:33,456
Hydramnios is not a typical feature, and the bowel may not be significantly dilated.

820
01:35:33,456 --> 01:35:40,681
A transverse view through the pelvis may show an enlarged rectum as an anechoic structure between the bladder and the sacrum.

821
01:35:40,681 --> 01:35:44,669
Anal atresia is a feature of the VACTERL association.

822
01:35:44,669 --> 01:35:47,469
# Ventral Wall Defects.

823
01:35:47,469 --> 01:35:54,069
The integrity of the abdominal wall is assessed at the level of the cord insertion during the standard examination .

824
01:35:54,069 --> 01:36:00,094
Ventral wall defects include gastroschisis, omphalocele, and body stalk anomaly.

825
01:36:00,094 --> 01:36:02,619
Figure 15-50.

826
01:36:02,619 --> 01:36:04,731
Normal ventral wall.

827
01:36:04,731 --> 01:36:12,519
Transverse view of the abdomen demonstrating normal umbilical cord insertion and integrity of the anterior abdominal wall.

828
01:36:12,519 --> 01:36:19,619
Gastroschisis This is a full-thickness abdominal wall defect located to the right of the umbilical cord insertion.

829
01:36:19,619 --> 01:36:23,969
Bowel herniates through the defect into the amnionic cavity.

830
01:36:23,969 --> 01:36:28,681
Gastroschisis may be diagnosed as early as the late first trimester .

831
01:36:28,681 --> 01:36:36,669
The prevalence is approximately 1 in 2000 births (Jones, 2016; Nelson, 2015).

832
01:36:36,669 --> 01:36:47,581
Gastroschisis is the one major anomaly more common in fetuses of younger mothers, and the average maternal age is 20 years (Santiago-Muñoz, 2007).

833
01:36:47,581 --> 01:36:54,731
Coexisting bowel abnormalities such as jejunal atresia are found in approximately 15 percent of cases .

834
01:36:54,731 --> 01:37:05,406
Gastroschisis is not associated with aneuploidy, and the survival rate is 90 to 95 percent (Nelson, 2015; Raitio, 2020).

835
01:37:05,406 --> 01:37:08,144
Figure 15-51.

836
01:37:08,144 --> 01:37:10,119
Gastroschisis.

837
01:37:10,119 --> 01:37:25,044
Transverse views of the lower abdomen at 13 weeks’ gestation (A) and 18 weeks (B) depict multiple small bowel loops (B) that have herniated into the amnionic cavity through a defect to the right of the cord insertion (arrow).

838
01:37:25,044 --> 01:37:35,681
Fetal-growth restriction complicates gastroschisis in 15 to 40 percent of cases (Overcash, 2014; Santiago-Muñoz, 2007).

839
01:37:35,681 --> 01:37:44,094
Growth restriction does not appear to confer increased mortality (Nelson, 2015; Overcash, 2014).

840
01:37:44,094 --> 01:38:00,031
However, earlier gestational age at delivery does pose a risk for adverse outcome, and planned delivery at 36 to 37 weeks’ gestation has not been found to benefit the neonate (Al-Kaff, 2016; South, 2013).

841
01:38:00,031 --> 01:38:10,019
Omphalocele The birth prevalence of this anomaly approximates 1 in 3000 to 5000 (Canfield, 2006; Dolk, 2010).

842
01:38:10,019 --> 01:38:15,131
It develops when the lateral ectomesodermal folds fail to meet in the midline.

843
01:38:15,131 --> 01:38:23,244
Abdominal organs herniate into the base of the umbilical cord, covered only by a two-layered sac of amnion and peritoneum .

844
01:38:23,244 --> 01:38:28,731
More than half of cases are associated with other major anomalies or aneuploidy.

845
01:38:28,731 --> 01:38:32,819
Aneuploidy is particularly common with smaller defects .

846
01:38:32,819 --> 01:38:41,569
Omphalocele is also a component of several syndromes, including Beckwith–Wiedemann, cloacal exstrophy, and pentalogy of Cantrell.

847
01:38:41,569 --> 01:38:54,044
Neonatal survival approximates 90 percent for isolated cases and 80 percent in those with other structural abnormalities (Raitio, 2021; Springett, 2014).

848
01:38:54,044 --> 01:39:01,581
Isolated defects containing liver are typically delivered via cesarean to decrease the risk for fetal trauma and bleeding.

849
01:39:01,581 --> 01:39:04,331
Figure 15-52.

850
01:39:04,331 --> 01:39:06,206
Omphalocele.

851
01:39:06,206 --> 01:39:14,319
Transverse view of the abdomen showing a large, round, membranecovered ventral wall defect containing exteriorized liver.

852
01:39:14,319 --> 01:39:20,294
Body Stalk Anomaly Also known as limb-body-wall complex or cyllosoma, this rare,

853
01:39:20,452 --> 01:39:25,227
lethal anomaly is characterized by abnormal body wall formation.

854
01:39:25,177 --> 01:39:32,252
Typically, no abdominal wall is visible, and the abdominal organs extrude into the extraamnionic coelom .

855
01:39:32,252 --> 01:39:38,977
The body and placenta are closely approximated or fused, and the umbilical cord is extremely short.

856
01:39:38,977 --> 01:39:45,227
Acuteangle scoliosis is another feature, and amnionic bands are often identified.

857
01:39:45,227 --> 01:39:48,039
Figure 15-53.

858
01:39:48,039 --> 01:39:50,202
Body-stalk anomaly.

859
01:39:50,202 --> 01:39:54,739
A. This 15-week fetus has no visible abdominal wall.

860
01:39:54,739 --> 01:40:01,852
The thorax is disproportionately small compared with the head, and the small lower torso is deviated to the side.

861
01:40:01,852 --> 01:40:06,264
Arrows point to the large mass of extruded abdominal organs.

862
01:40:06,264 --> 01:40:09,127
B. Acute-angle scoliosis.

863
01:40:09,127 --> 01:40:12,839
Arrows depict the abnormal curvature of the spine.

864
01:40:12,839 --> 01:40:17,289
Reproduced with permission from Deirdre Snelson, RDMS.

865
01:40:17,289 --> 01:40:20,839
# Kidneys and Genitourinary Tract.

866
01:40:20,839 --> 01:40:28,939
The fetal kidneys are visible adjacent to the spine, frequently in the first trimester and routinely by 18 weeks’ gestation .

867
01:40:28,939 --> 01:40:36,989
The length of the kidney approximates 20 mm at 20 weeks and grows about 1.1 mm each week thereafter .

868
01:40:36,989 --> 01:40:39,789
Figure 15-54.

869
01:40:39,789 --> 01:40:42,614
Normal fetal kidneys and bladder.

870
01:40:42,614 --> 01:40:48,039
A. The kidneys are visible adjacent to the spine in this 29-week fetus.

871
01:40:48,039 --> 01:40:52,977
A small amount of urine is often visible within the renal pelvis (arrow).

872
01:40:52,977 --> 01:41:00,414
B. Normal fetal bladder, outlined by the two superior vesical arteries as they become the umbilical arteries.

873
01:41:00,414 --> 01:41:08,902
The fetal bladder is also readily seen in the second trimester as a round, anechoic structure in the anterior midline of the pelvis.

874
01:41:08,902 --> 01:41:18,214
With application of Doppler, the bladder is outlined by the two superior vesical arteries as they become the umbilical arteries of the umbilical cord .

875
01:41:18,214 --> 01:41:24,227
The fetal ureters and urethra are not visible sonographically unless abnormally dilated.

876
01:41:24,227 --> 01:41:29,927
After 18 weeks’ gestation, the kidneys are the major source of amnionic fluid .

877
01:41:29,927 --> 01:41:38,214
Normal amnionic fluid volume in the second half of pregnancy suggests urinary tract patency and at least one functioning kidney.

878
01:41:38,214 --> 01:41:48,002
External genitalia are a component of the detailed examination and are part of the standard examination in multifetal gestations and when medically indicated .

879
01:41:48,002 --> 01:41:54,114
Identification may aid counseling in pregnancies at risk for an X-linked genetic condition.

880
01:41:54,114 --> 01:42:00,352
Examples of normal and ambiguous genitalia are shown in Figure 15-55.

881
01:42:00,352 --> 01:42:05,964
Disorders of sexual development are discussed in Chapter 3 (p. 35).

882
01:42:05,964 --> 01:42:08,789
Figure 15-55.

883
01:42:08,789 --> 01:42:11,764
Normal and ambiguous genitalia.

884
01:42:11,764 --> 01:42:14,027
A. Female labia.

885
01:42:14,027 --> 01:42:16,577
B. Male penis and scrotum.

886
01:42:16,577 --> 01:42:21,089
C. Ambiguous genitalia with a phallus and bifid scrotum.

887
01:42:21,089 --> 01:42:29,652
D. With this condition, color Doppler can assist locating the urethral meatus, which is pinpointed here by the urine stream (blue).

888
01:42:29,652 --> 01:42:39,027
Renal Pelvis Dilatation Present in 1 to 5 percent of fetuses, this finding is also called urinary tract dilatation or hydronephrosis.

889
01:42:39,027 --> 01:42:54,289
In 40 to 90 percent of cases, and particularly when mild, renal pelvis dilatation is transient or physiological and does not represent an underlying abnormality (Ismaili, 2003; Nguyen, 2010).

890
01:42:54,289 --> 01:43:01,552
In approximately one third of cases, a urinary tract abnormality is confirmed in the neonatal period.

891
01:43:01,552 --> 01:43:10,189
Of these, ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction and vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) are the most frequent.

892
01:43:10,189 --> 01:43:16,189
The fetal renal pelvis diameter is measured anterior-to-posterior in a transverse plane.

893
01:43:16,189 --> 01:43:20,214
Calipers are placed on the inner border of the fluid collection .

894
01:43:20,214 --> 01:43:36,752
Although various thresholds have been defined, the pelvis is typically considered dilated if it exceeds 4 mm in the second trimester or 7 mm at approximately 32 weeks’ gestation (Nguyen, 2014; Reddy, 2014).

895
01:43:36,752 --> 01:43:44,039
The second-trimester threshold is used to identify pregnancies that warrant subsequent third-trimester evaluation.

896
01:43:44,039 --> 01:43:46,914
Figure 15-56.

897
01:43:46,914 --> 01:43:49,402
Renal pelvis dilatation.

898
01:43:49,402 --> 01:43:58,064
A. The anterior-posterior diameter of the renal pelves measured 7 mm in the transverse plane in this 34-week fetus.

899
01:43:58,064 --> 01:44:08,227
B. Sagittal image at 32 weeks depicts renal pelvis dilatation with rounded calyces (arrow) in the setting of ureteropelvic junction obstruction.

900
01:44:08,227 --> 01:44:21,002
The Society for Fetal Urology categorized renal pelvis dilatation based on a metaanalysis of more than 100,000 screened pregnancies (Lee, 2006; Nguyen, 2010).

901
01:44:21,002 --> 01:44:24,777
Provided that the finding is isolated, the degree

902
01:44:24,940 --> 01:44:29,927
of dilation correlates with the likelihood of an underlying abnormality.

903
01:44:29,877 --> 01:44:37,977
Associated calyceal dilation, cortical thinning, or dilation elsewhere along the urinary tract confer increased risk .

904
01:44:37,977 --> 01:44:53,177
Mild pyelectasis in the second trimester is a minor aneuploidy marker associated with a slightly increased risk for trisomy 21, particularly when other findings or risk factors are also present (Fig. 17-3, p.

905
01:44:53,177 --> 01:44:54,865
340).

906
01:44:54,865 --> 01:44:57,402
Table 15-2.

907
01:44:57,402 --> 01:45:05,415
Risk for Postnatal Urinary Abnormality According to Degree of Renal Pelvis Dilation Ureteropelvic Junction Obstruction.

908
01:45:05,415 --> 01:45:11,277
This condition is the most common abnormality associated with renal pelvis dilatation.

909
01:45:11,277 --> 01:45:22,315
The birth prevalence is 1 in 1000 to 2000, and males are affected three times more often than females (Williams, 2007; Woodward, 2002).

910
01:45:22,315 --> 01:45:29,365
Obstruction is generally functional rather than anatomical, and it is bilateral in up to a fourth of cases.

911
01:45:29,365 --> 01:45:38,427
The likelihood of UPJ obstruction ranges from 5 percent with mild renal pelvis dilatation to more than 50 percent with severe dilatation .

912
01:45:38,427 --> 01:45:41,340
Duplicated Renal Collecting System.

913
01:45:41,340 --> 01:45:49,627
In this anatomical anomaly, the upper and lower poles of the kidney—called moieties—are each drained by a separate ureter .

914
01:45:49,627 --> 01:46:05,190
Duplication is found in approximately 1 in 4000 pregnancies, is more common in females, and is bilateral in 15 to 20 percent of cases (James, 1998; Vergani, 1998; Whitten, 2001).

915
01:46:05,190 --> 01:46:11,115
Sonographically, an intervening tissue band separates two distinct renal pelves.

916
01:46:11,115 --> 01:46:22,752
Hydronephrosis or ureteral dilation may develop due to abnormal implantation of one or both ureters within the bladder—a relationship that reflects the anatomical Weigert-Meyer rule.

917
01:46:22,752 --> 01:46:36,365
The upper pole ureter tends to develop obstruction from a ureterocele within the bladder, whereas the lower pole ureter has a shortened intravesical segment that predisposes to VUR (see Fig. 15-57).

918
01:46:36,365 --> 01:46:43,615
Thus, both moieties may become dilated from different etiologies, and both are at risk for loss of function.

919
01:46:43,615 --> 01:46:46,477
Figure 15-57.

920
01:46:46,477 --> 01:46:49,390
Duplicated renal collecting system.

921
01:46:49,390 --> 01:47:00,377
A. Renal pelvis dilatation is visible in both the upper (U) and lower (L) pole moieties, which are separated by an intervening band of renal tissue (arrowhead).

922
01:47:00,377 --> 01:47:05,015
B. A ureterocele (arrowhead) is visible within the bladder.

923
01:47:05,015 --> 01:47:08,977
Color Doppler depicts the superior vesical arteries.

924
01:47:08,977 --> 01:47:27,815
Renal Agenesis The estimated prevalence of bilateral renal agenesis is 1 in 8000 births, whereas that of unilateral renal agenesis is 1 in 1000 births (Cragan, 2009; Dolk, 2010; Sheih, 1989; Wiesel, 2005).

925
01:47:27,815 --> 01:47:35,765
With an absent kidney, color Doppler imaging of the descending aorta demonstrates absence of the ipsilateral renal artery .

926
01:47:35,765 --> 01:47:44,015
In addition, the ipsilateral adrenal gland typically enlarges to fill the renal fossa, termed the lying down adrenal sign .

927
01:47:44,015 --> 01:47:46,752
Figure 15-58.

928
01:47:46,752 --> 01:47:48,952
Renal agenesis.

929
01:47:48,952 --> 01:47:58,802
A. Coronal image of a fetus with bilateral renal agenesis in which color Doppler of the abdominal aorta is used to demonstrate absence of the renal arteries.

930
01:47:58,802 --> 01:48:09,565
B. In this fetus with unilateral renal agenesis, arrowheads point to the adrenal gland (arrowheads) filling the renal fossa, which is the “lying-down” adrenal sign.

931
01:48:09,565 --> 01:48:20,690
If renal agenesis is bilateral, no urine is produced, and the resulting anhydramnios leads to pulmonary hypoplasia, limb contractures, and distinctive facies.

932
01:48:20,690 --> 01:48:30,177
When this combination results from renal agenesis, it is called Potter syndrome, after Dr. Edith Potter, who described it in 1946.

933
01:48:30,177 --> 01:48:44,740
When these abnormalities result from severely decreased amnionic fluid volume from another etiology, such as bilateral multicystic dysplastic kidney or autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease, it is called Potter sequence.

934
01:48:44,740 --> 01:48:48,840
The prognosis for these abnormalities is extremely poor.

935
01:48:48,840 --> 01:48:55,577
Multicystic Dysplastic Kidney This severe form of renal dysplasia results in a nonfunctioning kidney.

936
01:48:55,577 --> 01:49:04,615
The nephrons and collecting ducts form abnormally, such that primitive ducts are surrounded by fibromuscular tissue, and the ureter is atretic .

937
01:49:04,615 --> 01:49:15,452
Sonographically, the kidney contains numerous, variably sized, smoothwalled cysts that do not communicate with the renal pelvis and are surrounded by echogenic cortex .

938
01:49:15,452 --> 01:49:18,265
Figure 15-59.

939
01:49:18,265 --> 01:49:20,890
Multicystic dysplastic

940
01:49:21,052 --> 01:49:22,664
kidneys.

941
01:49:22,614 --> 01:49:33,064
Coronal view of the fetal abdomen demonstrates enlarged kidneys that are filled with cysts of varying size and contain no visible renal pelvis or normalappearing renal tissue.

942
01:49:33,064 --> 01:49:40,489
Unilateral multicystic dysplastic kidney (MCDK) has a prevalence of 1 in 4000 births.

943
01:49:40,489 --> 01:49:48,864
Contralateral renal abnormalities are present in 30 to 40 percent—most frequently VUR or UPJ obstruction .

944
01:49:48,864 --> 01:49:53,639
Nonrenal anomalies have been reported in 25 percent of cases.

945
01:49:53,639 --> 01:50:02,552
Cystic dysplasia may occur as a component of many genetic syndromes (Lazebnik, 1999; Schreuder, 2009).

946
01:50:02,552 --> 01:50:08,177
If MCDK is isolated and unilateral, the prognosis is generally good.

947
01:50:08,177 --> 01:50:13,514
Bilateral MCDK is found in approximately 1 in 12,000 births.

948
01:50:13,514 --> 01:50:19,652
It is associated with severely decreased amnionic fluid volume starting early in gestation.

949
01:50:19,652 --> 01:50:23,627
This leads to Potter sequence and a poor prognosis.

950
01:50:23,627 --> 01:50:36,727
Polycystic Kidney Disease Of the hereditary polycystic diseases, only the infantile form of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD) may be reliably diagnosed prenatally.

951
01:50:36,727 --> 01:50:47,264
ARPKD is a chronic, progressive disease of the kidneys and liver that results in cystic dilation of the renal collecting ducts and in congenital hepatic fibrosis .

952
01:50:47,264 --> 01:50:57,214
The carrier frequency of a disease-causing mutation in the PKHD1 gene approximates 1 in 70, and the birth prevalence is 1 in 20,000 .

953
01:50:57,214 --> 01:51:08,427
The phenotypes of ARPKD range from lethal pulmonary hypoplasia at birth to a presentation in late childhood or even adulthood with predominantly hepatic manifestations.

954
01:51:08,427 --> 01:51:18,402
Sonographically, ARPKD displays abnormally large kidneys that may fill or even distend the fetal abdomen and have a solid, ground-glass texture .

955
01:51:18,402 --> 01:51:22,777
Severe oligohydramnios confers a poor prognosis.

956
01:51:22,777 --> 01:51:25,264
Figure 15-60.

957
01:51:25,264 --> 01:51:30,077
Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD).

958
01:51:30,077 --> 01:51:37,214
Transverse (A) and coronal (B) images of a 29-week fetus with marked renal enlargement.

959
01:51:37,214 --> 01:51:42,427
The kidneys measure 7 cm in length and have a ground-glass appearance.

960
01:51:42,427 --> 01:51:44,927
There is anhydramnios.

961
01:51:44,927 --> 01:51:53,814
Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD), which is far more common, usually does not manifest until adulthood .

962
01:51:53,814 --> 01:52:03,827
Even so, some fetuses with ADPKD have mild renal enlargement and enhanced renal echogenicity in the setting of normal amnionic fluid volume.

963
01:52:03,827 --> 01:52:11,289
The differential diagnosis for these findings includes several genetic syndromes, aneuploidy, or normal variant.

964
01:52:11,289 --> 01:52:21,514
Bladder Outlet Obstruction Distal obstruction of the urinary tract is more frequent in male fetuses, and the most common etiology is posterior urethral valves.

965
01:52:21,514 --> 01:52:26,177
It may be diagnosed as early as the first trimester in some cases .

966
01:52:26,177 --> 01:52:33,702
The bladder is markedly dilated, with accompanying dilation of the proximal urethra, which is termed the “keyhole” sign.

967
01:52:33,702 --> 01:52:41,802
Oligohydramnios, particularly before midpregnancy, portends pulmonary hypoplasia and poor prognosis.

968
01:52:41,802 --> 01:52:50,889
The kidneys may initially manifest severe hydroureteronephrosis but later develop cystic renal dysplasia or become small and echogenic.

969
01:52:50,889 --> 01:52:55,227
Outcome may be poor even with normal amnionic fluid volume.

970
01:52:55,227 --> 01:53:08,164
Associated anomalies occur in 40 percent of cases, and aneuploidy has been reported in 5 to 8 percent (Hayden, 1988; Hobbins, 1984; Mann, 2010).

971
01:53:08,164 --> 01:53:16,627
Evaluation and potential fetal therapy for bladder outlet obstruction is discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 376).

972
01:53:16,627 --> 01:53:19,427
Figure 15-61.

973
01:53:19,427 --> 01:53:21,802
Bladder outlet obstruction.

974
01:53:21,802 --> 01:53:27,877
A. The bladder (B) is so large that it fills the abdomen in this 13-week fetus.

975
01:53:27,877 --> 01:53:33,389
B. By 18 weeks, the kidneys (K) have become brightly echogenic.

976
01:53:33,389 --> 01:53:41,664
C. The bladder is markedly dilated and thick-walled, with dilation of the proximal urethra, termed the “keyhole” sign (arrow).

977
01:53:41,664 --> 01:53:43,377
Skeleton.

978
01:53:43,377 --> 01:53:50,314
The standard examination includes demonstrating the presence of the arms, legs, hands, and feet .

979
01:53:50,314 --> 01:53:57,202
The detailed examination includes, in addition, the number and position of the digits—fingers and toes .

980
01:53:57,202 --> 01:54:00,002
Figure 15-62.

981
01:54:00,002 --> 01:54:02,139
Normal extremities.

982
01:54:02,139 --> 01:54:05,064
A. Footprint with toes identified.

983
01:54:05,064 --> 01:54:07,577
B. Normal ankle position.

984
01:54:07,577 --> 01:54:10,577
C. Hand with fingers identified.

985
01:54:10,577 --> 01:54:13,702
D. Normal hand and forearm position.

986
01:54:13,702 --> 01:54:27,927
The Nosology and Classification of Genetic Skeletal Disorders includes 461 skeletal anomalies in 42 groups, characterized according to their molecular phenotype, clinical features, or radiographic

987
01:54:28,084 --> 01:54:29,809
findings .

988
01:54:29,759 --> 01:54:39,071
More than 90 percent of skeletal disorders now have a known genetic basis, with pathogenic variants identified in more than 400 genes.

989
01:54:39,071 --> 01:54:51,571
The two types of skeletal dysplasias are osteochondrodysplasias—the generalized abnormal development of bone and/or cartilage, and dysostoses—which are abnormalities of individual bones.

990
01:54:51,571 --> 01:55:02,259
In addition to these malformations, skeletal abnormalities include deformations, as with some cases of clubfoot, and disruptions such as limb-reduction defects.

991
01:55:02,259 --> 01:55:05,109
# Skeletal Dysplasias.

992
01:55:05,109 --> 01:55:10,421
The prevalence of skeletal dysplasias approximates 3 in 10,000 births.

993
01:55:10,421 --> 01:55:22,734
Two groups account for more than half of all cases: the fibroblast growth factor 3 (FGFR3) chondrodysplasia group and the osteogenesis imperfecta and decreased bone density group.

994
01:55:22,734 --> 01:55:26,646
Each occurs in 0.8 in 10,000 births .

995
01:55:26,646 --> 01:55:39,909
Evaluation of a pregnancy with suspected skeletal dysplasia includes a survey of every long bone, as well as the hands and feet, skull size and shape, clavicles, scapulae, thorax, and spine.

996
01:55:39,909 --> 01:55:48,571
Reference tables are used to determine which long bones are affected and ascertain the degree of shortening (Appendix, p. 1239).

997
01:55:48,571 --> 01:56:02,821
Involvement of all long bones is termed micromelia, whereas predominant involvement of only the proximal, intermediate, or distal long bone segments is termed rhizomelia, mesomelia, and acromelia, respectively.

998
01:56:02,821 --> 01:56:08,309
The degree of ossification should be noted, as should presence of bowing or fractures.

999
01:56:08,309 --> 01:56:17,346
Although precise characterization may elude prenatal diagnosis, it is frequently possible to determine whether a skeletal dysplasia is lethal.

1000
01:56:17,346 --> 01:56:35,734
Lethal dysplasias show profound long bone shortening, often with measurements far below the 5th percentile, and display femur length-to-abdominal circumference ratios <16 percent (Nelson, 2014; Rahemtullah, 1997; Ramus, 1998).

1001
01:56:35,734 --> 01:56:39,759
Other sonographic abnormalities are generally evident.

1002
01:56:39,759 --> 01:56:58,646
Pulmonary hypoplasia is suggested by a thoracic circumference <80 percent of the abdominal circumference value, a thoracic circumference <2.5th percentile, and a cardiac circumference >50 percent of the thoracic circumference value (Appendix, p. 1238).

1003
01:56:58,646 --> 01:57:03,546
Affected pregnancies may also develop hydramnios and/or hydrops.

1004
01:57:03,546 --> 01:57:11,396
The FGFR3 chondrodysplasias include achondroplasia, hypochondroplasia, and thanatophoric dysplasia.

1005
01:57:11,396 --> 01:57:15,971
Achondroplasia is the most common nonlethal skeletal dysplasia.

1006
01:57:15,971 --> 01:57:23,021
An impressive 98 percent of cases are due to a specific point mutation in the FGFR3 gene.

1007
01:57:23,021 --> 01:57:29,084
Inheritance is autosomal dominant, and 80 percent of cases result from a new mutation.

1008
01:57:29,084 --> 01:57:43,284
Achondroplasia is characterized by long bone shortening that is predominantly rhizomelic, an enlarged head with frontal bossing, depressed nasal bridge, exaggerated lumbar lordosis, and a trident configuration of the hands.

1009
01:57:43,284 --> 01:57:45,984
Intelligence is usually normal.

1010
01:57:45,984 --> 01:57:52,596
The femur and humerus measurements may not fall below the 5th percentile until the early third trimester.

1011
01:57:52,596 --> 01:58:03,146
In homozygotes, who represent 25 percent of the offspring of heterozygous parents, the condition is characterized by greater long bone shortening and is lethal.

1012
01:58:03,146 --> 01:58:11,171
The other major class of FGFR3 dysplasias, thanatophoric dysplasia, is the most common lethal skeletal disorder.

1013
01:58:11,171 --> 01:58:24,271
It is characterized by severe micromelia, and affected fetuses—particularly those with type II—may develop a characteristic cloverleaf skull deformity (Kleeblattschädel) due to craniosynostosis.

1014
01:58:24,271 --> 01:58:27,196
Genetic testing is confirmatory.

1015
01:58:27,196 --> 01:58:34,059
Osteogenesis imperfecta represents a group of skeletal dysplasias typified by hypomineralization.

1016
01:58:34,059 --> 01:58:43,759
There are multiple types, and more than 90 percent of cases are characterized by a mutation in the COL1A1 or COL1A2 gene.

1017
01:58:43,759 --> 01:58:47,771
Type II, also called the perinatal form, is lethal.

1018
01:58:47,771 --> 01:58:57,759
The skull displays a profound lack of ossification, and gentle pressure on the maternal abdomen from the ultrasound transducer results in visible skull deformation .

1019
01:58:57,759 --> 01:59:02,984
Other features include multiple in-utero fractures and ribs that appear “beaded.

1020
01:59:02,984 --> 01:59:10,446
Inheritance is autosomal dominant, such that all cases result from either new mutations or gonadal mosaicism.

1021
01:59:10,446 --> 01:59:15,146
Another skeletal dysplasia that creates severe hypomineralization

1022
01:59:15,316 --> 01:59:20,178
is hypophosphatasia, which has an autosomal recessive inheritance.

1023
01:59:20,128 --> 01:59:22,953
Figure 15-63.

1024
01:59:22,953 --> 01:59:25,603
Post-axial polydactyly.

1025
01:59:25,603 --> 01:59:31,941
A. In this image of the hand, the arrow points to a rudimentary digit adjacent to the little finger.

1026
01:59:31,941 --> 01:59:36,291
B. Foot with 6 toes in a fetus with trisomy 13.

1027
01:59:36,291 --> 01:59:39,203
The arrow points to the extra digit.

1028
01:59:39,203 --> 01:59:41,641
# Polydactyly.

1029
01:59:41,641 --> 01:59:49,166
The most common skeletal abnormality is polydactyly, which occurs in approximately 1 in 1000 births.

1030
01:59:49,166 --> 01:59:56,041
It is post-axial if on the side of the ulna or fibula and pre-axial if on the side of the radius or tibia .

1031
01:59:56,041 --> 02:00:03,553
The extra digit is often rudimentary, and in the absence of a bony component, prenatal detection may be limited.

1032
02:00:03,553 --> 02:00:10,391
Post-axial polydactyly is more common and is frequently inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion.

1033
02:00:10,391 --> 02:00:16,203
Polydactyly is also a feature of syndromes such as Meckel-Gruber and trisomy 13.

1034
02:00:16,203 --> 02:00:19,028
Figure 15-64.

1035
02:00:19,028 --> 02:00:22,941
Osteogenesis imperfecta, Type IIa.

1036
02:00:22,941 --> 02:00:34,966
A. Due to lack of skull ossification, gentle ultrasound transducer pressure on the maternal abdomen results in visible deformation (flattening) of the skull in this 24-week fetus.

1037
02:00:34,966 --> 02:00:40,428
B. When the transducer pressure is removed, the skull shape returns to normal.

1038
02:00:40,428 --> 02:00:47,778
C. In the four-chamber view of the heart, in-utero fractures lead to abrupt angulation of the ribs (arrows).

1039
02:00:47,778 --> 02:00:53,028
D. The thorax is markedly smaller than the abdomen in this sagittal image.

1040
02:00:53,028 --> 02:00:56,678
# Clubfoot—Talipes Equinovarus.

1041
02:00:56,678 --> 02:01:01,753
This disorder is notable for a deformed talus and shortened Achilles tendon.

1042
02:01:01,753 --> 02:01:10,528
The affected foot is abnormally fixed and positioned with equinus (downward pointing), varus (inward rotation), and forefoot adduction.

1043
02:01:10,528 --> 02:01:16,466
Most cases are considered malformations, with a multifactorial genetic component.

1044
02:01:16,466 --> 02:01:24,928
However, an association with environmental factors and with early amniocentesis suggests that deformation also plays a role .

1045
02:01:24,928 --> 02:01:30,378
Sonographically, the footprint is visible in the same plane as the tibia and fibula .

1046
02:01:30,378 --> 02:01:33,228
Figure 15-65.

1047
02:01:33,228 --> 02:01:36,316
Talipes equinovarus or clubfoot.

1048
02:01:36,316 --> 02:01:42,603
This condition is diagnosed by visualizing the “footprint” in the same plane as the tibia and fibula.

1049
02:01:42,603 --> 02:01:53,541
The prevalence of clubfoot approximates 1 in 1000 births, and the male:female ratio is 2:1 (Carey, 2003; Pavone, 2012).

1050
02:01:53,541 --> 02:01:58,203
Clubfoot is bilateral in approximately 50 percent of cases.

1051
02:01:58,203 --> 02:02:13,466
At least 50 percent of affected individuals have associated abnormalities, such as neural-tube defects, arthrogryposis, and myotonic dystrophy and other genetic syndromes (Mammen, 2004; Sharma, 2011).

1052
02:02:13,466 --> 02:02:18,616
If there are other abnormalities, aneuploidy is present in 30 percent.

1053
02:02:18,616 --> 02:02:28,591
In contrast, the aneuploidy rate is <4 percent when clubfoot appears isolated (Lauson, 2010; Sharma, 2011).

1054
02:02:28,591 --> 02:02:36,903
Thus, a careful search for associated abnormalities is warranted, and chromosomal microarray analysis may be considered.

1055
02:02:36,903 --> 02:02:39,766
# Limb-Reduction Defects.

1056
02:02:39,766 --> 02:02:44,978
Documentation of the arms and legs is a component of the standard examination.

1057
02:02:44,978 --> 02:02:51,166
The absence or hypoplasia of all or part of one or more extremities is a limb-reduction defect.

1058
02:02:51,166 --> 02:03:00,641
The birth prevalence is 4 to 8 in 10,000 (Kucik, 2012; Stoll, 2010; Vasluian, 2013).

1059
02:03:00,641 --> 02:03:15,828
Approximately half of these are isolated defects, up to one third occur as part of a recognized syndrome, and individuals in the remaining cases have other coexisting anomalies (Stoll, 2010; Vasluian, 2013).

1060
02:03:15,828 --> 02:03:20,191
Upper extremities are affected more frequently than lower ones.

1061
02:03:20,191 --> 02:03:26,053
A terminal transverse limb defect lacks part or all of a distal limb to create a stump .

1062
02:03:26,053 --> 02:03:34,853
This is more common than a longitudinal defect, which is complete or partial absence of the long bone(s) on only one side of a given extremity.

1063
02:03:34,853 --> 02:03:37,753
Figure 15-66.

1064
02:03:37,753 --> 02:03:40,566
Transverse limb-reduction defect.

1065
02:03:40,566 --> 02:03:45,766
A. At 18 weeks’ gestation, only a rudimentary hand was visible.

1066
02:03:45,766 --> 02:03:53,728
B. By 24 weeks, the radius and ulna were normal in size and appearance, and small rudimentary digits were evident.

1067
02:03:53,728 --> 02:03:57,578
Absence of an entire extremity is termed amelia.

1068
02:03:57,578 --> 02:04:06,166
Phocomelia, associated with thalidomide exposure, is an absence of one or more long bones with the hands or feet attached to the trunk .

1069
02:04:06,166 --> 02:04:16,641
Limb-reduction defects are associated with numerous genetic syndromes, such as Roberts syndrome, an autosomal recessive condition characterized by tetraphocomelia.

1070
02:04:16,641 --> 02:04:20,353
A clubhand deformity, usually from an absent

1071
02:04:20,524 --> 02:04:31,174
radius, is a component of the thrombocytopenia-absent radius syndrome and is also associated with trisomy 18 (Fig. 16-5, p. 312).

1072
02:04:31,124 --> 02:04:37,311
Limb-reduction defects may occur in the setting of a disruption such as amnionic-band sequence .

1073
02:04:37,311 --> 02:04:47,274
They have also been associated with chorionic villus sampling when performed before 10 weeks’ gestation (Fig. 17-5, p. 346).

